add(
  "chemistry",
  "matter_separation",
  "Heterogeneous Mixtures",

  `
  <h2>Heterogeneous Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Heterogeneous Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>heterogeneous mixture</strong> is a mixture whose
    composition is not uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p>
    Different portions of a heterogeneous mixture can contain different
    amounts or proportions of its components. The components may be
    visibly distinguishable, but visible differences are not required
    in every heterogeneous mixture.
  </p>

  <p>
    The substances in a heterogeneous mixture are physically combined.
    They do not have to react chemically to form a new compound.
  </p>

  <h3>Recognising Heterogeneous Mixtures</h3>

  <p>
    A useful way to identify a heterogeneous mixture is to consider
    whether different parts of the sample have different compositions.
    For example, a sample of soil may contain mineral particles,
    organic matter, and other components distributed unevenly.
  </p>

  <p>
    In some mixtures, different components are easy to see. In others,
    the mixture may look uniform to the naked eye, even though its
    composition differs between microscopic regions.
  </p>

  <h3>Examples</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sand and water:</strong> sand particles remain separate
    from the water and may settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Oil and water:</strong> under ordinary conditions,
    the liquids form separate layers when allowed to settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Soil:</strong> different particles and materials are
    distributed unevenly.</li>
    <li><strong>Granite:</strong> different mineral grains can often
    be seen in the rock.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Sand and Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A container holds sand and water.
  Explain why the contents form a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains sand and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine their distribution.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sand does not dissolve in water under ordinary conditions. Its
    particles remain separate from the water and may settle at the bottom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the definition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Different portions can contain different proportions of sand and
    water, so the composition is not uniform throughout the sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Sand and water form a heterogeneous
  mixture because the sand remains separate and is not distributed
  uniformly throughout the water.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Oil and Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Oil and water are poured into a
  transparent container. After standing, two layers appear. Explain
  why this is a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The components are oil and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Examine their behaviour.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Under ordinary conditions, oil and water do not mix into one
    uniform liquid. They separate into distinct layers.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Compare the composition of the layers.</strong></p>
  <p>
    One layer is mainly oil, while the other is mainly water.
    Their compositions are different.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oil and water form a heterogeneous
  mixture because the two components form separate layers with
  different compositions.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Granite</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A piece of granite contains visible
  grains of different minerals. Explain why it is a heterogeneous mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Observe the sample.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The granite contains visibly different mineral grains.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Consider the composition of different regions.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Different parts of the rock can contain different minerals and
    different proportions of those minerals.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the material.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample is not uniform in composition throughout.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Granite is a heterogeneous mixture of
  minerals because its different regions can have different compositions.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A heterogeneous mixture has a non-uniform composition.</li>
    <li>Different portions of the sample can contain different
    proportions of components.</li>
    <li>Its components may be visibly distinct, but visibility alone
    is not the definition.</li>
    <li>The components are physically combined rather than necessarily
    chemically combined into a new compound.</li>
    <li>Examples include sand and water, oil and water, soil, and granite.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    For each answer, identify the components and explain how their
    distribution determines whether the mixture is heterogeneous.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a heterogeneous mixture and explain what is meant by its composition being non-uniform.",
      hint: "Consider whether different portions contain the same proportions of components.",
      steps: [
        "State that a heterogeneous mixture contains more than one substance.",
        "Explain that its composition is not uniform throughout.",
        "Describe how different portions can differ in composition."
      ],
      ans: "A heterogeneous mixture is a mixture whose composition is not uniform throughout the sample. Different portions can contain different proportions of its components.",
      why: "The defining feature is non-uniform composition. The components may be visible, but visible separation is not essential to the definition."
    },
    {
      q: "A container contains sand and water. Explain why the mixture is heterogeneous even if the sand has settled at the bottom.",
      hint: "Consider the composition of the upper liquid and the material at the bottom.",
      steps: [
        "Identify sand and water as the components.",
        "Explain that the sand remains separate from the water.",
        "Compare the composition of the upper and lower portions."
      ],
      ans: "The mixture is heterogeneous because the upper portion is mainly water while the bottom contains accumulated sand. The components are not distributed uniformly throughout the sample.",
      why: "Settling makes the differences between the portions especially clear. The mixture remains a physical combination of sand and water."
    },
    {
      q: "Oil and water form two layers after standing. Explain what the layers tell you about the composition of the mixture.",
      hint: "Identify the main component in each layer.",
      steps: [
        "Identify oil and water as the two components.",
        "Describe the composition of the two layers.",
        "Connect the different compositions to the definition."
      ],
      ans: "One layer is mainly oil and the other is mainly water. Since the layers have different compositions, the sample is a heterogeneous mixture.",
      why: "A heterogeneous mixture does not have a uniform composition throughout. The separate layers demonstrate this non-uniformity."
    },
    {
      q: "A piece of granite contains different mineral grains. Explain why the composition of one region of the rock may differ from that of another region.",
      hint: "Think about the different minerals present in the grains.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the visible mineral grains.",
        "Explain that different regions may contain different minerals or proportions.",
        "Use the variation in composition to classify the sample."
      ],
      ans: "Different regions may contain different types and proportions of mineral grains. Granite is therefore a heterogeneous mixture because its composition is not uniform throughout.",
      why: "The different mineral components are physically present together, and their distribution varies across the rock."
    },
    {
      q: "Must every heterogeneous mixture have components that are easily visible to the naked eye? Explain your answer.",
      hint: "Distinguish between the definition of a heterogeneous mixture and one way of observing it.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the defining feature of a heterogeneous mixture.",
        "Consider whether composition can vary at a scale smaller than the naked eye can resolve.",
        "State whether visibility is essential."
      ],
      ans: "No. A heterogeneous mixture has a non-uniform composition, but its different components or regions do not always have to be distinguishable with the naked eye.",
      why: "Visible layers and particles can help identify heterogeneity, but the underlying criterion is variation in composition throughout the sample."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. IDENTIFYING PURE SUBSTANCES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "matter_separation",
  "Identifying Pure Substances",

  `
  <h2>Identifying Pure Substances</h2>

  <h3>Meaning of a Pure Substance</h3>

  <p>
    In chemistry, a <strong>pure substance</strong> is material made
    up of only one chemical substance, with a definite composition
    and characteristic properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance may be an element or a compound. An element contains
    one type of atom, while a compound contains different elements
    chemically combined in fixed proportions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A pure substance is not necessarily an element. Pure water is a
    compound, while pure copper is an element.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition and Identity</h3>

  <p>
    A pure substance has a definite chemical identity. A sample of
    pure water consists of water, H₂O, rather than water mixed with
    dissolved salt or other substances.
  </p>

  <p>
    If two samples are both pure samples of the same substance, their
    chemical composition is the same, even if the samples have different
    masses or volumes.
  </p>

  <h3>Using Melting Point and Boiling Point</h3>

  <p>
    At a specified pressure, a pure substance generally melts and boils
    at characteristic temperatures. For a pure crystalline substance
    at a given pressure, melting commonly occurs over a very narrow
    temperature range.
  </p>

  <p>
    Many mixtures melt or boil over a range of temperatures because
    their components have different physical properties. However, this
    is evidence rather than a universal test: some mixtures have
    characteristic phase-change behaviour, and pressure must be
    considered when comparing temperatures.
  </p>

  <p>
    A melting-point or boiling-point measurement should therefore be
    interpreted alongside other evidence about the sample's composition.
  </p>

  <h3>Purity and Appearance</h3>

  <p>
    A material that looks clear, colourless, or uniform is not
    automatically pure. Dissolved substances may be invisible, and
    some mixtures look uniform even though they contain more than one
    substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, a clear salt solution is a mixture of salt and water.
    Its appearance alone does not establish that it is a pure substance.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Pure Copper</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample is confirmed to contain only
  copper. Explain whether it is a pure substance and whether it is
  an element or a compound.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify its composition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains only copper atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the definition of purity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    It contains only one chemical substance, copper.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the substance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Copper is an element, not a compound, because it contains only
    one type of atom.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a pure substance and an
  element because it contains only copper.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Pure Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains only H₂O molecules.
  Explain why it is a pure substance but not an element.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Examine the chemical identity.</strong></p>
  <p>
    All the molecules in the sample are water molecules.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Determine whether the sample is pure.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since only one chemical substance is present, the sample is pure water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Determine whether it is an element.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Each water molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen, which are
    different elements chemically combined.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Pure water is a pure substance, but it
  is a compound rather than an element.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Interpreting Melting-Point Evidence</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> At the same specified pressure, Sample A
  melts over a very narrow temperature range, while Sample B melts
  over a broad range. What can this evidence suggest about the samples?</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Compare the observations.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A has a narrow melting range, while Sample B has a broad range.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Apply the usual behaviour of pure substances.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A pure crystalline substance commonly melts at a characteristic
    temperature under specified conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a cautious conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Sample A may be purer than Sample B, while Sample B may contain
    impurities or be a mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The observations suggest that Sample A
  may be purer and Sample B may contain impurities or multiple components.
  The melting data alone do not prove the identity or purity of either sample.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A pure substance contains only one chemical substance.</li>
    <li>Pure substances include elements and compounds.</li>
    <li>A pure substance has a definite chemical composition.</li>
    <li>A clear or uniform appearance does not prove purity.</li>
    <li>Melting-point and boiling-point behaviour can provide evidence
    about purity when pressure and measurement conditions are controlled.</li>
    <li>Experimental evidence should be interpreted carefully rather
    than treated as an infallible test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    For each sample, explain what is present and distinguish purity
    from whether the substance is an element or a compound.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define a pure substance and explain why a pure substance can be either an element or a compound.",
      hint: "Focus on the number of chemical substances present, not just the number of elements.",
      steps: [
        "Define a pure substance in terms of its chemical identity and composition.",
        "Describe the composition of an element.",
        "Describe the composition of a compound."
      ],
      ans: "A pure substance consists of only one chemical substance with a definite composition. It can be an element, which contains one type of atom, or a compound, which contains different elements chemically combined in fixed proportions.",
      why: "Purity means that only one chemical substance is present. It does not mean that the substance must contain only one type of element."
    },
    {
      q: "A sample contains only H₂O molecules. Explain why it is pure but is not an element.",
      hint: "Ask whether the sample contains one chemical substance and whether its molecules contain one type of atom.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the substance represented by H₂O.",
        "Determine whether another chemical substance is present.",
        "Identify the different elements within each water molecule."
      ],
      ans: "The sample is pure because it contains only water. It is not an element because each H₂O molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen chemically combined.",
      why: "Pure substances include compounds as well as elements. The presence of two different elements in each molecule makes water a compound."
    },
    {
      q: "A clear, colourless liquid is tested and found to contain dissolved salt. Can its appearance be used to identify it as a pure substance? Explain.",
      hint: "Consider whether a dissolved substance must be visible.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the substances present in the liquid.",
        "Consider whether dissolved salt can be visible.",
        "Use the actual composition rather than appearance to classify the sample."
      ],
      ans: "No. The liquid is a mixture of water and dissolved salt, even if it looks clear and colourless.",
      why: "Appearance alone cannot establish purity. A dissolved substance can be distributed throughout a liquid without being visible."
    },
    {
      q: "At the same pressure, a solid sample melts over a broad temperature range. Explain what this may suggest and why the observation is not conclusive by itself.",
      hint: "Compare the usual melting behaviour of a pure crystalline substance with that of many mixtures.",
      steps: [
        "Describe the melting behaviour observed.",
        "Explain what a broad melting range can suggest.",
        "State why additional evidence is needed."
      ],
      ans: "A broad melting range may suggest that the sample contains impurities or is a mixture. However, the observation alone does not prove its composition or identity.",
      why: "Many mixtures melt over a range of temperatures, while a pure crystalline substance commonly melts over a narrow range at a specified pressure. Experimental conditions and the nature of the material must also be considered."
    },
    {
      q: "A student says that every pure substance is an element. Use one example to show why this statement is incorrect.",
      hint: "Choose a pure compound and explain its composition.",
      steps: [
        "State what an element contains.",
        "Identify a pure compound.",
        "Explain why the compound is pure but not an element."
      ],
      ans: "The statement is incorrect. Pure water is a pure substance because it contains only H₂O, but it is a compound because each molecule contains hydrogen and oxygen.",
      why: "A pure substance is defined by having one chemical substance present. That substance may be an element or a compound."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. IDENTIFYING MIXTURES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "matter_separation",
  "Identifying Mixtures",

  `
  <h2>Identifying Mixtures</h2>

  <h3>How to Identify a Mixture</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>mixture</strong> contains two or more substances physically
    combined. The components retain their chemical identities and
    are not necessarily chemically combined into one new compound.
  </p>

  <p>
    To identify a mixture, investigate what substances are present,
    whether their proportions can vary, and whether they can be
    separated by suitable physical methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    No single observation is guaranteed to identify every mixture.
    A reliable conclusion should be based on the available evidence
    about the sample's composition and behaviour.
  </p>

  <h3>Clues That a Sample May Be a Mixture</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Two or more substances are known to be present.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary from one sample
    to another.</li>
    <li>Different components can be separated by a suitable physical method.</li>
    <li>The sample contains visibly different materials or layers,
    although a mixture can also look uniform.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Physical Separation as Evidence</h3>

  <p>
    Many mixtures can be separated because their components have
    different physical properties. For example, filtration can separate
    an insoluble solid from a liquid, and a magnet can remove a magnetic
    component from non-magnetic material.
  </p>

  <p>
    Separating a mixture does not require turning its components into
    different chemical substances. The method separates substances
    that were already present.
  </p>

  <h3>Composition Can Vary</h3>

  <p>
    The component proportions in a mixture are not fixed by a single
    chemical formula. Two salt solutions can contain different
    concentrations of salt and still be mixtures of salt and water.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mixture can be homogeneous, with uniform composition, or
    heterogeneous, with non-uniform composition. Therefore, a uniform
    appearance does not rule out a mixture.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Identifying Salt Water</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two samples of salt water contain
  different amounts of dissolved salt relative to the amount of water.
  Explain why both samples are mixtures.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Both samples contain sodium chloride and water.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Compare their compositions.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The relative amounts of salt and water differ between the samples.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Apply the definition.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The salt and water are physically combined, and their proportions
    can vary.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Both samples are mixtures because they
  contain salt and water in variable proportions rather than one
  chemically combined substance with a fixed formula.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Identifying Iron and Sand</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains iron filings and
  sand. A magnet removes the iron filings while the sand remains.
  Explain what this evidence tells you.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the components.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains iron and sand.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Interpret the separation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    A magnet attracts the iron filings but not the sand under ordinary
    conditions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Draw a conclusion.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The components were present as separate substances and could be
    separated using their different magnetic properties.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The sample is a mixture of iron and sand.
  Magnetic separation removes the iron without requiring the formation
  of new substances.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Identifying a Uniform Liquid</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A clear liquid looks uniform. Testing
  establishes that it contains water and a dissolved substance. Explain
  whether it can be a mixture despite its uniform appearance.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Consider the appearance.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The liquid appears uniform, but appearance alone does not establish purity.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Use the composition evidence.</strong></p>
  <p>
    The sample contains water and another dissolved substance.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Classify the sample.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Since more than one substance is physically combined in a uniform
    composition, the liquid is a homogeneous mixture.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. The sample can be a homogeneous
  mixture because it contains multiple substances even though it looks uniform.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 4: Distinguishing a Mixture from a Compound</h3>

  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A sample contains two substances
  physically combined, and each can be recovered using a suitable
  physical separation method. Explain why the evidence supports
  classifying the sample as a mixture.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the substances already present.</p>
  <p>
    Two components are present in the original sample.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider the separation.</p>
  <p>
    A suitable physical method separates the components without
    chemically decomposing one compound into its elements.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Apply the definition.</p>
  <p>
    The sample is a physical combination of substances.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The evidence supports classifying the
  sample as a mixture because its components were physically combined
  and can be separated using a suitable physical method.</p>

  <h3>Important Points</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A mixture contains at least two substances physically combined.</li>
    <li>Its components retain their chemical identities.</li>
    <li>The proportions of the components can vary.</li>
    <li>Mixtures may be homogeneous or heterogeneous.</li>
    <li>Some mixtures can be separated using physical properties such
    as solubility, particle size, boiling behaviour, or magnetism.</li>
    <li>A uniform appearance does not prove that a sample is pure.</li>
    <li>Use composition and suitable experimental evidence to classify
    an unknown sample.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diagnostic Questions</h3>

  <p>
    Use the evidence given in each question. Explain why the evidence
    supports the classification instead of relying on appearance alone.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "State three pieces of evidence that can help you identify a mixture.",
      hint: "Consider composition, proportions, and physical separation.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the number of substances present.",
        "Consider whether the component proportions can vary.",
        "Consider whether suitable physical methods can separate the components."
      ],
      ans: "Evidence includes finding two or more substances in the sample, observing that their proportions can vary, and showing that the components can be separated by a suitable physical method.",
      why: "These clues support the conclusion that the sample is a physical combination of substances. Not every clue must be available in every investigation."
    },
    {
      q: "Two salt solutions contain different concentrations of salt. Explain why both can be classified as mixtures.",
      hint: "Compare their components and the proportions in which those components are present.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the components in each solution.",
        "Compare the relative amounts of salt and water.",
        "Explain why variable proportions are consistent with a mixture."
      ],
      ans: "Both solutions contain sodium chloride and water. Their different concentrations show that the proportions can vary, so both are mixtures.",
      why: "Mixtures do not have a single fixed ratio of components. Changing the relative amounts of salt and water does not automatically create a different compound."
    },
    {
      q: "Iron filings are mixed with sand. A magnet removes the iron from the sample. Explain why this result supports classifying the original sample as a mixture.",
      hint: "Consider the identities of the materials before and after separation.",
      steps: [
        "Identify iron and sand as the original components.",
        "Explain why the magnet attracts the iron.",
        "Describe what the separation demonstrates about the components."
      ],
      ans: "The original sample is a mixture because it contains iron and sand as separate substances. Their different magnetic properties allow the iron to be removed without chemically decomposing a compound.",
      why: "The physical separation shows that the components retain their identities and can be separated by exploiting a difference in physical properties."
    },
    {
      q: "A clear liquid appears to contain only one substance, but testing confirms that it contains water and dissolved salt. Explain why its appearance is not enough to classify it as pure.",
      hint: "Consider what can happen when a substance dissolves completely.",
      steps: [
        "Describe the liquid's appearance.",
        "Identify the substances established by testing.",
        "Classify the sample using its composition rather than appearance."
      ],
      ans: "The liquid is a mixture because it contains both water and dissolved salt. Its uniform, clear appearance does not make it a pure substance.",
      why: "Dissolved components may not be visible. Composition is more reliable than appearance alone when identifying a mixture."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why a mixture can be homogeneous or heterogeneous, and state the difference between these two types.",
      hint: "Focus on the uniformity of composition throughout the sample.",
      steps: [
        "Define the uniformity of a homogeneous mixture.",
        "Define the uniformity of a heterogeneous mixture.",
        "Explain why both can still be mixtures."
      ],
      ans: "A homogeneous mixture has uniform composition throughout the sample, while a heterogeneous mixture has non-uniform composition. Both are mixtures because each contains two or more substances physically combined.",
      why: "The terms homogeneous and heterogeneous describe how the components are distributed, not whether the sample contains only one substance."
    }
  ]
);
