/* =========================================================
   5. VALENCY
========================================================= */

add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Valency",

  `
  <h2>Valency</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of valency</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Valency</b> is the combining capacity of an atom or group of atoms.
    In simple introductory examples, it is often determined by the number
    of electrons an atom loses, gains, or shares when forming bonds.
  </p>

  <p>
    Valency is not always identical to ionic charge or oxidation state.
    In this screen, use the simple combining-capacity model commonly
    applied to introductory chemical formulae.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Finding valency from electron arrangements</h3>
  <p>
    For many main-group elements, the outer-shell electron count helps
    predict common valencies.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>One outer electron: commonly valency 1, as in sodium.</li>
    <li>Two outer electrons: commonly valency 2, as in magnesium.</li>
    <li>Three outer electrons: commonly valency 3, as in aluminium.</li>
    <li>Five outer electrons: commonly valency 3 in simple covalent examples, as in nitrogen.</li>
    <li>Six outer electrons: commonly valency 2 in simple covalent examples, as in oxygen.</li>
    <li>Seven outer electrons: commonly valency 1 in simple examples, as in chlorine.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For elements with five, six, or seven outer electrons, the simple
    model often estimates how many electrons are needed to complete an
    outer shell. Some elements show variable valency, so these patterns
    must not be treated as universal.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Valency and the formation of ions</h3>
  <p>
    In common ionic examples, sodium forms Na⁺ and has valency 1;
    magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and has valency 2; aluminium forms Al³⁺ and
    has valency 3. The valency gives the magnitude of the combining
    capacity in these examples, while the ion's charge includes a sign.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Using valency to write formulae</h3>
  <p>
    A compound's formula must represent an electrically neutral
    combination of ions. The total positive charge must equal the
    total negative charge.
  </p>

  <p>
    For magnesium chloride, magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻.
    One Mg²⁺ ion requires two Cl⁻ ions to balance the charge, so the
    formula is MgCl₂.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find the common valency of magnesium</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium has electron arrangement 2,8,2. Determine its common valency in simple ionic compounds.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Count the outer-shell electrons. Magnesium has two.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Consider the common ion it forms. Magnesium can lose these two electrons to form Mg²⁺.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Determine the combining capacity from the number of electrons lost.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Magnesium commonly has valency 2 in simple ionic compounds.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Estimate the valency of oxygen in water</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons. In a simple covalent model, how many bonds does it commonly form?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen has six electrons in its outer shell.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> It needs two more electrons to reach eight in the simple shell model.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Oxygen can share one pair of electrons with each of two hydrogen atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Oxygen commonly has valency 2 in water, H₂O.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Use valency to write magnesium chloride's formula</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the formula of a compound formed from Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium has charge +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chloride ion has charge −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chloride ions give a total charge of 2 × (−1) = −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges balance: +2 + (−2) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Valency describes combining capacity. Use outer-shell electrons to
    predict common valencies, then check that any ionic formula has
    balanced total charge.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Magnesium has the electron arrangement 2,8,2. Determine its common valency in simple ionic compounds.",
      hint: "Count the outer-shell electrons and consider how many magnesium commonly loses.",
      steps: [
        "Magnesium has two outer-shell electrons.",
        "It commonly loses both electrons.",
        "It forms Mg²⁺, so its combining capacity in this example is 2."
      ],
      ans: "Valency 2.",
      why: "Magnesium commonly forms a 2+ ion by losing two outer-shell electrons."
    },
    {
      q: "An oxygen atom has six outer-shell electrons. Using the simple shell model, predict its common valency in water.",
      hint: "Work out how many electrons oxygen needs to complete an outer shell.",
      steps: [
        "Oxygen has six outer-shell electrons.",
        "It needs two more to reach eight in the simple model.",
        "In water, it forms two covalent bonds, one with each hydrogen atom."
      ],
      ans: "Valency 2.",
      why: "Oxygen forms two covalent bonds in water, H₂O."
    },
    {
      q: "Use the ions Mg²⁺ and Cl⁻ to determine the formula of magnesium chloride.",
      hint: "The total positive and negative charges must cancel.",
      steps: [
        "One Mg²⁺ ion contributes a charge of +2.",
        "One Cl⁻ ion contributes a charge of −1.",
        "Two chloride ions contribute −2.",
        "The total charge is +2 − 2 = 0."
      ],
      ans: "MgCl₂.",
      why: "Two chloride ions are needed to balance the charge of one magnesium ion."
    },
    {
      q: "Aluminium commonly forms Al³⁺ ions. What is its common valency in this ionic example, and how many electrons does a neutral aluminium atom lose to form Al³⁺?",
      hint: "Use the magnitude of the charge and remember that a positive ion forms by losing electrons.",
      steps: [
        "The aluminium ion has a charge of +3.",
        "The magnitude of this charge is 3.",
        "A neutral aluminium atom must lose three electrons to form Al³⁺."
      ],
      ans: "Valency 3; three electrons are lost.",
      why: "Losing three negatively charged electrons leaves the aluminium ion with three more protons than electrons."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why the formula for aluminium oxide is Al₂O₃ when the ions are Al³⁺ and O²⁻.",
      hint: "Find the smallest numbers of each ion that give equal total positive and negative charges.",
      steps: [
        "Each Al³⁺ ion has charge +3.",
        "Each O²⁻ ion has charge −2.",
        "Two aluminium ions give 2 × (+3) = +6.",
        "Three oxide ions give 3 × (−2) = −6.",
        "The charges cancel, giving a neutral compound."
      ],
      ans: "Al₂O₃.",
      why: "Two aluminium ions and three oxide ions give a total charge of zero."
    }
  ]
);


/* =========================================================
   6. IONIC BONDING
========================================================= */

add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Ionic Bonding",

  `
  <h2>Ionic Bonding</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an ion?</h3>
  <p>
    An <b>ion</b> is an atom or group of atoms with an overall electric
    charge because the number of electrons differs from the number
    of protons.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A positive ion, or cation, has fewer electrons than protons.</li>
    <li>A negative ion, or anion, has more electrons than protons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Definition of ionic bonding</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Ionic bonding</b> is the strong electrostatic attraction between
    oppositely charged ions. Electron transfer often produces the ions,
    but the bond itself is the attraction between the charges.
  </p>

  <h3>3. How ionic bonding forms</h3>
  <p>
    Consider sodium chloride. A sodium atom loses one electron and
    becomes Na⁺. A chlorine atom gains that electron and becomes Cl⁻.
    The opposite charges attract.
  </p>

  <p>
    The electron-transfer equations are:
  </p>
  <p><b>Sodium:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>
  <p><b>Chlorine:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <p>
    One electron is lost by sodium and gained by chlorine. Electrons
    are transferred; they are not created or destroyed in the process.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Ionic compounds form giant lattices</h3>
  <p>
    Solid sodium chloride is not made of separate NaCl molecules.
    Its ions are arranged in a repeating three-dimensional ionic lattice.
    Each ion is surrounded by ions of the opposite charge. The
    electrostatic attractions extend throughout the structure.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Formulae and charge balance</h3>
  <p>
    An ionic compound must have an overall charge of zero. For example,
    Mg²⁺ combines with two Cl⁻ ions to form MgCl₂. The ratio is one
    magnesium ion to two chloride ions because +2 + 2(−1) = 0.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Explain the ionic bond in sodium chloride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Describe how sodium chloride forms from sodium and chlorine atoms.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium loses one electron: Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Chlorine gains that electron: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The ions have opposite charges: Na⁺ is positive and Cl⁻ is negative.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrostatic attraction holds the ions together in the ionic lattice.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Sodium transfers one electron to chlorine, forming Na⁺ and Cl⁻. Their electrostatic attraction produces ionic bonding.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the formula of calcium chloride</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. Determine the formula of calcium chloride.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> One calcium ion contributes +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chloride ion contributes −1.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chloride ions contribute a total of −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The charges balance: +2 + 2(−1) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> CaCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Explain the formula MgO</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Explain why the formula is MgO.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium contributes a charge of +2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Oxide contributes a charge of −2.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> One of each ion gives +2 + (−2) = 0.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> MgO, because the ions combine in a 1:1 ratio to produce a neutral compound.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Electron transfer forms ions in many ionic compounds. Ionic bonding
    is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions,
    which are arranged in a giant lattice in the solid.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define ionic bonding.",
      hint: "State what is attracting what, and include the charges.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the particles: ions.",
        "Identify the charges: one type is positive and the other negative.",
        "State the force: electrostatic attraction."
      ],
      ans: "Ionic bonding is the strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions.",
      why: "The attraction between opposite charges holds the ions together."
    },
    {
      q: "Sodium transfers one electron to chlorine. Write the ions formed and explain why they attract.",
      hint: "Determine the charge on each atom after electron transfer.",
      steps: [
        "Sodium loses one electron and forms Na⁺.",
        "Chlorine gains one electron and forms Cl⁻.",
        "Na⁺ has a positive charge and Cl⁻ has a negative charge.",
        "Opposite charges attract."
      ],
      ans: "Na⁺ and Cl⁻ form; they attract because they have opposite charges.",
      why: "This electrostatic attraction is the ionic bond in sodium chloride."
    },
    {
      q: "Calcium forms Ca²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. Calculate the number of chloride ions needed for one calcium ion.",
      hint: "Each chloride ion contributes −1, while calcium contributes +2.",
      steps: [
        "One calcium ion has charge +2.",
        "One chloride ion has charge −1.",
        "Two chloride ions give 2 × (−1) = −2.",
        "The total charge is +2 − 2 = 0."
      ],
      ans: "Two chloride ions; the formula is CaCl₂.",
      why: "The formula must represent an electrically neutral compound."
    },
    {
      q: "Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and oxygen forms O²⁻. Determine the simplest formula and show the charge calculation.",
      hint: "Check whether one ion of each type balances the charges.",
      steps: [
        "Mg²⁺ contributes +2.",
        "O²⁻ contributes −2.",
        "Add the charges: +2 + (−2) = 0.",
        "The ions combine in a 1:1 ratio."
      ],
      ans: "MgO; the total charge is zero.",
      why: "Equal and opposite charges cancel with one magnesium ion and one oxide ion."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why solid sodium chloride is described as a giant ionic lattice rather than a collection of separate NaCl molecules.",
      hint: "Think about the arrangement of ions throughout the solid.",
      steps: [
        "Sodium chloride contains Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions.",
        "The ions form a repeating three-dimensional arrangement.",
        "Each ion is attracted to surrounding ions of the opposite charge.",
        "The electrostatic attraction extends throughout the structure."
      ],
      ans: "Solid sodium chloride contains a repeating three-dimensional arrangement of Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions held by attractions throughout the lattice, rather than separate NaCl molecules.",
      why: "The formula NaCl gives the simplest ratio of ions in the lattice, not the identity of an isolated molecule."
    }
  ]
);


/* =========================================================
   7. ELECTRON TRANSFER
========================================================= */

add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Electron Transfer",

  `
  <h2>Electron Transfer</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of electron transfer</h3>
  <p>
    <b>Electron transfer</b> is the movement of one or more electrons
    from one particle to another. In many introductory examples of
    ionic bonding, a metal atom loses electrons and a non-metal atom
    gains them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Losing electrons forms a positive ion. Gaining electrons forms a
    negative ion. The number of protons in the nucleus does not change
    during ordinary ion formation.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Electron loss</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral sodium atom contains 11 protons and 11 electrons.
    When it loses one electron, it still has 11 protons but only
    10 electrons. Its net charge becomes +1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <h3>3. Electron gain</h3>
  <p>
    A neutral chlorine atom contains 17 protons and 17 electrons.
    When it gains one electron, it has 17 protons and 18 electrons.
    Its net charge becomes −1.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Electrons are conserved</h3>
  <p>
    In a transfer between sodium and chlorine, the electron lost by
    sodium is the electron gained by chlorine. The transfer must be
    accounted for on both sides. It does not mean that the electron
    disappears or that a new electron is created.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Predicting the number of electrons transferred</h3>
  <p>
    The common ion formed by an atom helps identify the number of
    electrons transferred. Magnesium commonly loses two electrons
    to form Mg²⁺. Oxygen commonly gains two electrons to form O²⁻.
    Their charges balance in magnesium oxide, MgO.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Write the electron-loss equation for sodium</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write an equation showing a sodium atom forming a sodium ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Sodium loses one electron.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Write Na on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Write Na⁺ and the released electron e⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The total charge on the right is +1 − 1 = 0, matching the neutral atom on the left.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Write the electron-gain equation for oxygen</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation for a neutral oxygen atom gaining two electrons to form an oxide ion.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Oxygen gains two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Place O and two electrons on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Place O²⁻ on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The left side has total charge −2 and the right side has charge −2.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Balance electron transfer between magnesium and chlorine</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium forms Mg²⁺ and chlorine forms Cl⁻. How many chlorine atoms are needed to accept the electrons lost by one magnesium atom?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Magnesium loses two electrons: Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each chlorine atom gains one electron: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Two chlorine atoms are needed to accept the two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> The overall transfer is Mg + 2Cl → Mg²⁺ + 2Cl⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Two chlorine atoms accept the two electrons lost by one magnesium atom. The resulting ion ratio is 1:2, giving MgCl₂.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Track electrons explicitly. Electron loss produces positive ions,
    electron gain produces negative ions, and the number of electrons
    lost must equal the number gained in the transfer.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Write the equation for a sodium atom losing one electron to form Na⁺.",
      hint: "An electron lost by the atom appears as a product.",
      steps: [
        "Write the neutral sodium atom: Na.",
        "Write the sodium ion formed: Na⁺.",
        "Add the released electron e⁻ to the products.",
        "Check charge: +1 − 1 = 0."
      ],
      ans: "Na → Na⁺ + e⁻.",
      why: "The equation shows that sodium loses one electron and becomes positively charged."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the equation for an oxygen atom gaining two electrons to form O²⁻.",
      hint: "Electrons gained must appear on the reactant side.",
      steps: [
        "Write the oxygen atom as O.",
        "Place two electrons on the left: 2e⁻.",
        "Write the oxide ion on the right: O²⁻.",
        "Check that the charge is −2 on both sides."
      ],
      ans: "O + 2e⁻ → O²⁻.",
      why: "Gaining two electrons gives oxygen two more electrons than protons and produces a 2− ion."
    },
    {
      q: "Magnesium loses two electrons. Write its electron-loss equation and state the ion formed.",
      hint: "Show both electrons on the product side.",
      steps: [
        "Start with a neutral magnesium atom, Mg.",
        "Show the loss of two electrons.",
        "Write the resulting ion with charge +2."
      ],
      ans: "Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻; the ion is Mg²⁺.",
      why: "Losing two negatively charged electrons leaves magnesium with a net charge of +2."
    },
    {
      q: "One magnesium atom loses two electrons. Each chlorine atom gains one electron. How many chlorine atoms are required to accept both electrons?",
      hint: "Divide the two electrons released by the one electron accepted per chlorine atom.",
      steps: [
        "Magnesium releases two electrons.",
        "Each chlorine atom accepts one electron.",
        "Two electrons ÷ one electron per chlorine atom = two chlorine atoms."
      ],
      ans: "Two chlorine atoms.",
      why: "The transfer produces one Mg²⁺ ion and two Cl⁻ ions, corresponding to MgCl₂."
    },
    {
      q: "A neutral atom has 12 protons and 12 electrons. It loses two electrons. Determine its new charge and explain why its proton number stays unchanged.",
      hint: "Count the electrons remaining and compare them with the 12 protons.",
      steps: [
        "The atom begins with 12 protons and 12 electrons.",
        "After losing two electrons, it has 10 electrons.",
        "The net charge is 12 positive charges plus 10 negative charges, giving +2.",
        "Ordinary electron transfer changes the electron count, not the number of protons in the nucleus."
      ],
      ans: "The ion has charge +2. It has 12 protons and 10 electrons.",
      why: "An ion forms by gaining or losing electrons. Its element identity remains the same because the number of protons is unchanged."
    }
  ]
);


/* =========================================================
   8. FORMATION OF POSITIVE IONS
========================================================= */

add(
  "chemistry",
  "chemical_bonding",
  "Formation of Positive Ions",

  `
  <h2>Formation of Positive Ions</h2>

  <h3>1. What is a positive ion?</h3>
  <p>
    A <b>positive ion</b>, also called a <b>cation</b>, is an atom or
    group of atoms with an overall positive charge. A positive ion forms
    when the particle loses one or more electrons.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Why does electron loss produce a positive charge?</h3>
  <p>
    Protons have a charge of +1 and electrons have a charge of −1.
    In a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number of
    electrons. If electrons are removed, there are more protons than
    electrons, so the remaining particle is positively charged.
  </p>

  <p>
    The charge of an ion can be found using:
  </p>
  <p><b>Ion charge = number of protons − number of electrons</b></p>

  <h3>3. Formation of sodium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Sodium has 11 protons and 11 electrons. It loses one electron
    to form Na⁺. The ion has 11 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Na → Na⁺ + e⁻</p>

  <h3>4. Formation of magnesium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Magnesium has 12 protons and 12 electrons. It commonly loses two
    electrons to form Mg²⁺. The ion has 12 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <h3>5. Formation of aluminium ions</h3>
  <p>
    Aluminium has 13 protons and 13 electrons. It commonly loses three
    electrons to form Al³⁺. The ion has 13 protons and 10 electrons.
  </p>
  <p><b>Equation:</b> Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻</p>

  <h3>6. Do positive ions lose protons?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Ordinary chemical ion formation involves electrons, not changes
    to the nucleus. Sodium remains sodium after losing an electron
    because it still has 11 protons. If the number of protons changed,
    the atom would become a different element.
  </p>

  <h3>Worked example 1: Find the charge of a sodium ion</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> An ion contains 11 protons and 10 electrons. Calculate its charge.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Each proton contributes +1, so the total proton charge is +11.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Each electron contributes −1, so the total electron charge is −10.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Net charge = 11 − 10 = +1.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> The ion has charge +1, written Na⁺ if it is a sodium ion.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 2: Determine the electrons in Mg²⁺</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Magnesium has atomic number 12. How many electrons are in Mg²⁺?</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Atomic number 12 means magnesium has 12 protons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> A neutral magnesium atom has 12 electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> The 2+ charge means it has lost two electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 4:</b> Electrons remaining = 12 − 2 = 10.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Mg²⁺ has 10 electrons and 12 protons.</p>

  <h3>Worked example 3: Write the equation for aluminium ion formation</h3>
  <p><b>Question:</b> Write the equation showing aluminium forming Al³⁺.</p>
  <p><b>Step 1:</b> Aluminium loses three electrons.</p>
  <p><b>Step 2:</b> Put the neutral aluminium atom on the left.</p>
  <p><b>Step 3:</b> Put Al³⁺ and three electrons on the right.</p>
  <p><b>Answer:</b> Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻.</p>
  <p><b>Check:</b> The right-side charge is +3 − 3 = 0, equal to the charge of the neutral atom on the left.</p>

  <h3>Key idea</h3>
  <p>
    Positive ions form when electrons are lost. The proton number remains
    unchanged, and the positive charge equals the number of electrons
    lost by a neutral atom.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "An ion has 11 protons and 10 electrons. Calculate its charge and write the charge notation.",
      hint: "Subtract the number of electrons from the number of protons.",
      steps: [
        "Number of protons = 11.",
        "Number of electrons = 10.",
        "Charge = 11 − 10 = +1."
      ],
      ans: "Charge +1; the ion is written Na⁺ if it is sodium.",
      why: "One more proton than electron gives a net positive charge of +1."
    },
    {
      q: "Magnesium has atomic number 12. Determine the number of electrons in Mg²⁺.",
      hint: "A neutral atom has electrons equal to its atomic number. A 2+ ion has lost two electrons.",
      steps: [
        "A neutral magnesium atom has 12 electrons.",
        "Mg²⁺ has lost two electrons.",
        "Electrons remaining = 12 − 2 = 10."
      ],
      ans: "10 electrons.",
      why: "The +2 charge indicates that magnesium has two fewer electrons than protons."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the equation for the formation of Al³⁺ from a neutral aluminium atom.",
      hint: "The atom loses three electrons, which appear on the product side.",
      steps: [
        "Start with Al.",
        "Write the aluminium ion Al³⁺.",
        "Add three released electrons, 3e⁻.",
        "Check that +3 − 3 = 0."
      ],
      ans: "Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻.",
      why: "The equation conserves both the aluminium atom and total charge."
    },
    {
      q: "A neutral atom contains 13 protons and 13 electrons. It loses three electrons. State the number of protons, the number of electrons, and the resulting charge.",
      hint: "Only the electron count changes during ordinary ion formation.",
      steps: [
        "The number of protons remains 13.",
        "Electrons remaining = 13 − 3 = 10.",
        "Net charge = 13 − 10 = +3."
      ],
      ans: "13 protons, 10 electrons, charge +3.",
      why: "Losing three electrons leaves three more protons than electrons, producing a 3+ ion."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner says that sodium becomes a different element when it forms Na⁺ because it loses an electron. Explain the error.",
      hint: "Recall what determines an element's identity.",
      steps: [
        "An element's identity is determined by its number of protons.",
        "Sodium has 11 protons before ion formation.",
        "Losing an electron changes the electron count, not the proton count.",
        "The particle still has 11 protons and is therefore still sodium."
      ],
      ans: "Sodium remains sodium because it still has 11 protons. Losing an electron forms a sodium ion, Na⁺, rather than a new element.",
      why: "Ion formation changes electrical charge; changing the proton number would change the element."
    }
  ]
);

