add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Properties of Acids",
  `
  <h2>Properties of Acids</h2>

  <h3>1. What is an acid?</h3>

  <p>
    An <strong>acid</strong> is a substance that produces hydrogen ions,
    H<sup>+</sup>, when dissolved in water. These ions are responsible
    for the characteristic acidic properties of aqueous solutions.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when hydrogen chloride dissolves in water, it forms
    hydrochloric acid:
  </p>

  <p>
    HCl(aq) → H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    This equation is a simplified school-level representation of
    ionisation in water. Hydrogen ions associate with water molecules
    to form hydronium ions, H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Characteristic properties</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Effect on indicators:</strong> acids turn blue litmus paper red.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>pH:</strong> an acidic aqueous solution has a pH below 7
      under the usual school-level conditions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Electrical conductivity:</strong> aqueous acids conduct
      electricity because they contain mobile ions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reactions:</strong> acids react with certain metals,
      carbonates, hydrogen carbonates and basic metal oxides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    These properties are observed in aqueous solutions. Do not assume
    that every acid has the same concentration, reacts at the same rate,
    or produces identical observations in every reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying an acid using litmus</strong></p>

  <p>
    A colourless solution turns blue litmus paper red. What does this
    observation suggest?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the characteristic litmus test.</p>
  <p>
    Acids turn blue litmus red, whereas alkalis turn red litmus blue.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the observation.</p>
  <p>
    Since blue litmus turns red, the observation is consistent with an
    acidic solution.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The solution is acidic, subject to confirming
  the test conditions and identity of the sample.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Interpreting pH</strong></p>

  <p>
    A solution has a pH of 3. Is it acidic, neutral or alkaline?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use the usual school-level pH classification.</p>
  <p>
    pH below 7 is acidic, pH 7 is neutral, and pH above 7 is alkaline.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Compare the given value with 7.</p>
  <p>
    Since 3 is less than 7, the solution is acidic.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Acidic.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Explaining conductivity</strong></p>

  <p>
    Why can aqueous hydrochloric acid conduct electricity?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particles in solution.</p>
  <p>
    Hydrochloric acid forms mobile ions in water, including H<sup>+</sup>
    in the simplified school-level representation and Cl<sup>−</sup>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Connect ions to electrical conduction.</p>
  <p>
    These charged particles move through the solution and carry electric
    charge.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Aqueous hydrochloric acid conducts electricity
  because it contains mobile ions.</p>

  <h3>4. Safety</h3>

  <p>
    Never taste an acid to identify it. Some acids are corrosive.
    Avoid skin and eye contact, use appropriate protective equipment,
    and follow laboratory instructions.
  </p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Define an acid in terms of the ions it produces in water.",
      hint: "Identify the characteristic positive ion associated with acids.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the school-level definition of an acid.",
        "State the ion produced in aqueous solution."
      ],
      ans: "An acid is a substance that produces hydrogen ions, H+, in aqueous solution.",
      why: "Hydrogen ions are responsible for the characteristic acidic properties of aqueous solutions."
    },
    {
      q: "A solution turns blue litmus paper red. Explain what this observation indicates.",
      hint: "Recall the characteristic colour change caused by acids.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the original colour of the litmus paper.",
        "Recall the effect of an acid on blue litmus.",
        "Use the observed colour change to classify the solution."
      ],
      ans: "The observation indicates that the solution is acidic.",
      why: "Acids turn blue litmus paper red. The test indicates acidity but does not, by itself, identify the particular acid."
    },
    {
      q: "A solution has a pH of 4. Classify the solution and justify your answer.",
      hint: "Compare the pH value with 7.",
      steps: [
        "Recall that a pH below 7 is acidic under the usual school-level classification.",
        "Compare 4 with 7.",
        "State the classification."
      ],
      ans: "The solution is acidic because its pH of 4 is below 7.",
      why: "The pH scale distinguishes acidic, neutral and alkaline aqueous solutions."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why aqueous hydrochloric acid conducts electricity.",
      hint: "Think about the charged particles present in the solution.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the ions formed when hydrogen chloride dissolves in water.",
        "Recognise that the ions are mobile in solution.",
        "Explain how moving charged particles carry current."
      ],
      ans: "Aqueous hydrochloric acid conducts electricity because it contains mobile ions that carry electric charge.",
      why: "Electrical conduction in the solution depends on the movement of charged particles, not simply on the presence of water."
    },
    {
      q: "Give two characteristic properties of an aqueous acid and explain each briefly.",
      hint: "Choose from indicator behaviour, pH and electrical conductivity.",
      steps: [
        "State one characteristic property.",
        "Explain the observation associated with it.",
        "State a second property and explain it."
      ],
      ans: "An aqueous acid turns blue litmus red because of its acidic nature. It has a pH below 7 under the usual school-level conditions. It also conducts electricity because it contains mobile ions.",
      why: "These properties help identify acidic solutions without tasting or directly handling them."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SCREEN 2: REACTIONS OF ACIDS WITH METALS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Reactions of Acids with Metals",
  `
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Metals</h2>

  <h3>1. The general reaction</h3>

  <p>
    A sufficiently reactive metal reacts with a suitable dilute acid to
    form a <strong>salt and hydrogen gas</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Metal + Acid → Salt + Hydrogen</p>

  <p>
    The salt formed depends on the metal and the acid used. For example,
    hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while dilute sulfuric acid
    forms sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not every metal reacts with dilute acids in the same way. Metals
    below hydrogen in the reactivity series, such as copper, do not
    normally displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid or dilute
    sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Recognising hydrogen gas</h3>

  <p>
    A reacting metal may produce bubbles of gas. To test whether the
    gas is hydrogen, collect a small sample safely and bring a lighted
    splint to the mouth of the test tube as instructed by the teacher.
    Hydrogen burns with a characteristic squeaky pop.
  </p>

  <p>
    Bubbles alone do not prove that the gas is hydrogen. The gas must
    be tested appropriately.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Magnesium and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for magnesium reacting with dilute
    hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Predict the products.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium forms magnesium chloride, and hydrogen gas is released.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mg + HCl → MgCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Balance chlorine and hydrogen.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Magnesium chloride contains two chlorine atoms, so place 2 before HCl.
    This also gives two hydrogen atoms, which form one H<sub>2</sub> molecule.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Zinc and sulfuric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the products when zinc reacts with dilute sulfuric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> The salt is zinc sulfate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Hydrogen gas is the other product.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Zn(s) + H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) →
    ZnSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Identifying the gas</strong></p>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid. A gas is collected
    and burns with a squeaky pop when tested with a lighted splint.
    Identify the gas and explain your answer.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Recall the reaction pattern.</p>
  <p>
    A reactive metal reacting with a suitable dilute acid produces a
    salt and hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the test.</p>
  <p>
    The squeaky-pop test is the characteristic test for hydrogen.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas is hydrogen.</p>

  <h3>4. Observations</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Bubbles may form as gas is released.</li>
    <li>The metal may gradually dissolve.</li>
    <li>The reaction may warm the mixture.</li>
    <li>The rate depends on the metal, acid concentration, temperature and surface area.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Carry out gas tests only with small quantities under teacher
    supervision, away from flames during gas collection.
  </p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "State the general word equation for the reaction between a sufficiently reactive metal and a suitable dilute acid.",
      hint: "Name the two products.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the reactants: a reactive metal and a suitable dilute acid.",
        "Recall the salt formed from the metal and acid.",
        "Name the gas produced."
      ],
      ans: "Metal + Acid → Salt + Hydrogen",
      why: "Reactive metals can displace hydrogen from suitable dilute acids, forming a salt and hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      q: "Write and balance the equation for magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "The products are magnesium chloride and hydrogen gas.",
      steps: [
        "Write the unbalanced equation: Mg + HCl → MgCl2 + H2.",
        "Balance chlorine by placing 2 before HCl.",
        "Check that magnesium, hydrogen and chlorine atoms balance."
      ],
      ans: "Mg + 2HCl → MgCl2 + H2",
      why: "There is one magnesium atom, two chlorine atoms and two hydrogen atoms on each side of the balanced equation."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the balanced equation for zinc reacting with dilute sulfuric acid.",
      hint: "The salt is zinc sulfate.",
      steps: [
        "Write the reactants: Zn and H2SO4.",
        "Predict zinc sulfate and hydrogen as the products.",
        "Check the atom counts on both sides."
      ],
      ans: "Zn + H2SO4 → ZnSO4 + H2",
      why: "One zinc atom, one sulfate group and two hydrogen atoms are present on each side."
    },
    {
      q: "A gas produced when magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid burns with a squeaky pop. Identify the gas and state the test observation.",
      hint: "Recall the characteristic test for hydrogen.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the products of a reactive metal and dilute acid.",
        "Identify the gas that gives the squeaky-pop test.",
        "State the gas and observation."
      ],
      ans: "The gas is hydrogen. It burns with a squeaky pop when tested with a lighted splint.",
      why: "The squeaky-pop test is the characteristic test for hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why copper normally does not produce hydrogen when placed in dilute hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "Use the reactivity series.",
      steps: [
        "Recall that a metal must be sufficiently reactive to displace hydrogen from the dilute acid.",
        "Locate copper relative to hydrogen in the reactivity series.",
        "Connect its position to the expected observation."
      ],
      ans: "Copper is below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so it does not normally displace hydrogen from dilute hydrochloric acid.",
      why: "The usual metal-acid reaction requires a metal that is sufficiently reactive to displace hydrogen from the acid."
    }
  ]
);


