add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Reactions of Acids with Carbonates",
  `
  <h2>Reactions of Acids with Carbonates</h2>

  <h3>1. The reaction pattern</h3>

  <p>
    Acids react with metal carbonates to produce a <strong>salt, water
    and carbon dioxide gas</strong>.
  </p>

  <p><strong>General word equation:</strong></p>

  <p>Acid + Metal Carbonate → Salt + Water + Carbon Dioxide</p>

  <p>
    The salt depends on the acid and the metal in the carbonate.
    Hydrochloric acid forms chloride salts, while sulfuric acid forms
    sulfate salts.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Testing the gas</h3>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide can be tested by passing it through clear limewater.
    The limewater turns milky because insoluble calcium carbonate forms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Carbon dioxide + Calcium hydroxide → Calcium carbonate + Water
  </p>

  <p>
    CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub>(aq) →
    CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)
  </p>

  <h3>3. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calcium carbonate and hydrochloric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Write the balanced equation for calcium carbonate reacting with
    hydrochloric acid.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Identify the salt.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Calcium carbonate contains calcium, and hydrochloric acid supplies
    chloride ions. The salt is calcium chloride.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Write all the products.</strong></p>

  <p>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub> + HCl →
    CaCl<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Balance the equation.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Place 2 before HCl to supply two chlorine atoms and two hydrogen atoms.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2HCl(aq) →
    CaCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Sodium carbonate and nitric acid</strong></p>

  <p>
    Predict the salt and write the balanced equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sodium carbonate contains sodium, so the salt is sodium nitrate.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> The other products are water and carbon dioxide.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>Balanced equation:</strong>
    Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2HNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) →
    2NaNO<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)
  </p>

  <p>
    Check: both sides contain two sodium atoms, two nitrogen atoms,
    two hydrogen atoms, one carbon atom and six oxygen atoms.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Interpreting an observation</strong></p>

  <p>
    A carbonate is added to dilute acid. Bubbles form, and the gas turns
    limewater milky. Identify the gas and explain the evidence.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Carbonates reacting with acids release carbon dioxide.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The gas is carbon dioxide.</p>

  <h3>4. What the observations mean</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Effervescence indicates that gas is being released.</li>
    <li>Milky limewater supports the identification of carbon dioxide.</li>
    <li>The carbonate may gradually dissolve as the reaction proceeds.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Bubbles alone are not enough to identify the gas; use the appropriate
    test to support the conclusion.
  </p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "State the three products formed when an acid reacts with a metal carbonate.",
      hint: "One product is a salt, and the other two are water and a gas.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the general reaction pattern for acids and carbonates.",
        "Name the salt.",
        "Name the liquid product and the gaseous product."
      ],
      ans: "A salt, water and carbon dioxide.",
      why: "Carbonates react with acids to release carbon dioxide while forming water and a salt."
    },
    {
      q: "Write and balance the equation for calcium carbonate reacting with hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "The salt formed is calcium chloride.",
      steps: [
        "Write the products: calcium chloride, water and carbon dioxide.",
        "Write the unbalanced equation.",
        "Place 2 before HCl and check all atoms."
      ],
      ans: "CaCO3 + 2HCl → CaCl2 + H2O + CO2",
      why: "The equation is balanced because calcium, carbon, oxygen, hydrogen and chlorine atoms are equal on both sides."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the balanced equation for sodium carbonate reacting with nitric acid.",
      hint: "The salt formed is sodium nitrate.",
      steps: [
        "Write sodium nitrate, water and carbon dioxide as the products.",
        "Balance sodium by placing 2 before NaNO3.",
        "Balance nitric acid by placing 2 before HNO3.",
        "Check all atoms."
      ],
      ans: "Na2CO3 + 2HNO3 → 2NaNO3 + H2O + CO2",
      why: "The balanced equation conserves the numbers of sodium, nitrogen, hydrogen, carbon and oxygen atoms."
    },
    {
      q: "Carbon dioxide is passed through clear limewater. State the observation and explain why it occurs.",
      hint: "Think about the insoluble substance formed in limewater.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the test for carbon dioxide.",
        "Identify the product formed when carbon dioxide reacts with limewater.",
        "Relate the product to the observed colour or appearance."
      ],
      ans: "The limewater turns milky because insoluble calcium carbonate forms.",
      why: "The suspended calcium carbonate makes the limewater appear cloudy or milky."
    },
    {
      q: "A carbonate reacts with an acid and produces bubbles. Explain why the bubbles alone are insufficient to identify the gas.",
      hint: "Separate evidence of gas production from evidence of gas identity.",
      steps: [
        "State what bubbles demonstrate.",
        "Explain why bubbles do not specify the identity of a gas.",
        "Name the appropriate test for the gas expected from an acid-carbonate reaction."
      ],
      ans: "Bubbles show that a gas is being released but do not identify it. Pass the gas through limewater; carbon dioxide turns the limewater milky.",
      why: "A gas should be identified using a characteristic test rather than appearance alone."
    }
  ]
);


