add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Strong and Weak Bases",
  `
  <h2>Strong and Weak Bases</h2>

  <h3>1. What determines base strength?</h3>

  <p>
    The strength of a base describes how it behaves in water and how
    effectively it produces or accepts ions involved in basic
    behaviour. For common school-level examples, strong bases produce
    hydroxide ions extensively in aqueous solution, while weak bases
    react with water only partially.
  </p>

  <p>
    Base strength is different from concentration. Concentration
    describes how much substance is dissolved per unit volume.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Strong bases</h3>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide is a strong base. When it dissolves in water,
    it dissociates essentially completely into sodium ions and
    hydroxide ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NaOH(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    Potassium hydroxide behaves similarly:
  </p>

  <p>
    KOH(s) → K<sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <h3>3. Weak bases</h3>

  <p>
    Aqueous ammonia is a common example of a weak base. It reacts
    partially with water to form ammonium ions and hydroxide ions:
  </p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) ⇌
    NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>(aq) + OH<sup>−</sup>(aq)
  </p>

  <p>
    The double arrow indicates an equilibrium. Most dissolved ammonia
    remains as NH<sub>3</sub> rather than forming ions through this
    reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Strength versus concentration</h3>

  <p>
    A dilute solution of a strong base can have a lower hydroxide-ion
    concentration than a more concentrated solution of a weak base.
    The exact comparison depends on the concentrations and chemical
    equilibria involved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Therefore, the word strong does not automatically mean concentrated,
    and the word weak does not mean dilute.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Sodium hydroxide</strong></p>

  <p>
    Sodium hydroxide dissociates essentially completely in water.
    Explain what this means.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the ions formed.</p>
  <p>NaOH → Na<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup></p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the arrow.</p>
  <p>
    The single arrow represents essentially complete dissociation
    of dissolved sodium hydroxide into its ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The dissolved sodium hydroxide produces
  sodium and hydroxide ions essentially completely.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Aqueous ammonia</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why aqueous ammonia is classified as a weak base.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Examine its reaction with water.</p>

  <p>
    NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O ⇌
    NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>−</sup>
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Interpret the double arrow.</p>
  <p>
    The reaction is reversible, and only a proportion of the ammonia
    reacts with water to produce ions.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Aqueous ammonia is a weak base because
  its reaction with water is partial and establishes an equilibrium.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Strength and concentration</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner calls sodium hydroxide weak because its solution is
    dilute. Explain the error.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Strength describes the extent of the
  relevant ion formation or reaction.</p>
  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Dilute describes the amount of substance
  per unit volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The conclusion is incorrect. Sodium
  hydroxide is a strong base even when its solution is dilute.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Remember</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Sodium hydroxide is a strong base.</li>
    <li>Aqueous ammonia is a weak base.</li>
    <li>Strong or weak describes base behaviour, not solution concentration.</li>
  </ul>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Explain what is meant by a strong base using sodium hydroxide as an example.",
      hint: "Consider how sodium hydroxide behaves when dissolved in water.",
      steps: [
        "State that sodium hydroxide is a strong base.",
        "Write its dissociation equation.",
        "Explain what the equation says about ion formation."
      ],
      ans: "Sodium hydroxide is a strong base because it dissociates essentially completely in water to form Na+ and OH− ions: NaOH → Na+ + OH−.",
      why: "Strong bases such as sodium hydroxide produce their ions extensively in aqueous solution."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the equation for aqueous ammonia reacting with water and explain the double arrow.",
      hint: "The products are ammonium ions and hydroxide ions.",
      steps: [
        "Write NH3 and H2O as the reactants.",
        "Write NH4+ and OH− as the products.",
        "Use a double arrow to show reversible partial reaction."
      ],
      ans: "NH3(aq) + H2O(l) ⇌ NH4+(aq) + OH−(aq). The double arrow indicates a reversible reaction that establishes equilibrium.",
      why: "Aqueous ammonia is a weak base because only a proportion reacts with water to form ions."
    },
    {
      q: "Classify sodium hydroxide and aqueous ammonia as strong or weak bases.",
      hint: "Compare their behaviour in water.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the behaviour of sodium hydroxide in water.",
        "Recall the behaviour of ammonia with water.",
        "Classify each base."
      ],
      ans: "Sodium hydroxide is a strong base; aqueous ammonia is a weak base.",
      why: "Sodium hydroxide dissociates essentially completely, whereas ammonia reacts only partially with water to form ions."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why a dilute sodium hydroxide solution is still described as a solution of a strong base.",
      hint: "Separate concentration from base strength.",
      steps: [
        "Define concentration in terms of amount per unit volume.",
        "Recall the behaviour of sodium hydroxide in water.",
        "Explain why dilution does not change its classification as a strong base."
      ],
      ans: "Dilute describes the small amount of sodium hydroxide per unit volume. Sodium hydroxide remains a strong base because the dissolved compound dissociates essentially completely in water.",
      why: "Strength and concentration describe different properties; dilution does not turn sodium hydroxide into a weak base."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why the words strong and concentrated cannot be used interchangeably when describing bases.",
      hint: "One describes behaviour in water, and the other describes amount per volume.",
      steps: [
        "Define base strength.",
        "Define concentration.",
        "Give a reason why a strong base can be dilute."
      ],
      ans: "Strength describes the extent of ion formation or reaction in water, while concentration describes the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume. A strong base can be dilute.",
      why: "The two terms refer to different chemical properties."
    }
  ]
);


