add(
  "chemistry",
  "acids_bases_salts",
  "Titration",
  `
  <h2>Titration</h2>

  <h3>What Is Titration?</h3>
  <p><strong>Titration</strong> is a laboratory method used to determine the volume of one solution required to react completely with a measured volume of another solution.</p>

  <p>In an acid-base titration, an acid reacts with a base. An indicator helps show when the reaction has reached its endpoint, which is used to identify the appropriate volume of solution required.</p>

  <h3>Apparatus Used</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Burette:</strong> delivers a measured, variable volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Pipette:</strong> measures and transfers a fixed volume of solution.</li>
    <li><strong>Conical flask:</strong> holds the measured solution being titrated.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator:</strong> changes colour over a suitable pH range to help identify the endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>White tile:</strong> makes the indicator colour change easier to observe.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic Titration Procedure</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Rinse the pipette with the solution it will measure and use it to transfer a known volume into a conical flask.</li>
    <li>Add a few drops of a suitable indicator to the solution in the flask.</li>
    <li>Rinse the burette with the solution it will deliver, fill it, and record the initial burette reading.</li>
    <li>Add the burette solution to the flask while swirling the flask gently.</li>
    <li>As the endpoint approaches, add the solution drop by drop until the indicator shows a permanent endpoint colour change.</li>
    <li>Record the final burette reading.</li>
    <li>Calculate the volume delivered by subtracting the initial reading from the final reading.</li>
    <li>Repeat the titration to obtain closely agreeing results where required.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Calculating the Titre</h3>
  <p>The <strong>titre</strong> is the volume delivered from the burette during a titration.</p>

  <p><strong>Titre = final burette reading − initial burette reading</strong></p>

  <p>Burette readings are normally recorded in cm³. The titre is also expressed in cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 1: Calculating a Titre</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> The initial burette reading is 1.20 cm³ and the final reading is 24.60 cm³. Calculate the titre.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: titre = final reading − initial reading.</li>
    <li>Substitute the readings: titre = 24.60 − 1.20.</li>
    <li>Subtract: 24.60 − 1.20 = 23.40.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The titre is 23.40 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 2: Finding a Missing Final Reading</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> A titration has a titre of 22.75 cm³ and an initial burette reading of 0.50 cm³. Find the final reading.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Start with titre = final reading − initial reading.</li>
    <li>Rearrange: final reading = titre + initial reading.</li>
    <li>Substitute: final reading = 22.75 + 0.50.</li>
    <li>Add: 22.75 + 0.50 = 23.25.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The final burette reading is 23.25 cm³.</p>

  <h3>Worked Example 3: Comparing Two Titrations</h3>
  <p><strong>Question:</strong> Two titrations give titres of 24.20 cm³ and 24.30 cm³. Calculate their mean titre.</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Add the two titres: 24.20 + 24.30 = 48.50 cm³.</li>
    <li>Divide the total by the number of titres: 48.50 ÷ 2 = 24.25 cm³.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mean titre is 24.25 cm³.</p>
  <p>In an actual experiment, use the closely agreeing titres specified by the practical instructions. Do not automatically average results that differ substantially.</p>

  <h3>Understanding the Endpoint</h3>
  <p>The endpoint is the point at which the indicator changes colour according to the chosen indicator's transition range. A suitable indicator should be selected for the acid-base reaction being investigated.</p>

  <p>The endpoint is an experimental observation. It is used to estimate the equivalence point, where the reacting amounts are in the stoichiometric proportions required by the balanced chemical equation.</p>

  <h3>Check Your Understanding</h3>
  <p>Answer each question in writing. Show your calculations where necessary.</p>
  `,
  [
    {
      q: "Define titration and state what it is used to determine in an acid-base experiment.",
      hint: "Think about measured volumes and the point at which the reaction reaches its endpoint.",
      steps: [
        "Explain that titration involves reacting measured solutions.",
        "State that the volume delivered from one solution is measured.",
        "Explain that the result helps determine the volume required for the reaction."
      ],
      ans: "Titration is a laboratory method in which measured solutions are reacted to determine the volume of one solution required to react with a measured amount of another.",
      why: "Titration provides a measured volume that can be used to analyse the reaction between the solutions."
    },
    {
      q: "A burette reading is initially 0.85 cm³ and finally 23.40 cm³. Calculate the titre and show your working.",
      hint: "Subtract the initial reading from the final reading.",
      steps: [
        "Write titre = final reading − initial reading.",
        "Substitute: 23.40 − 0.85.",
        "Calculate the difference."
      ],
      ans: "Titre = 23.40 − 0.85 = 22.55 cm³.",
      why: "The titre is the volume delivered, not simply the final burette reading."
    },
    {
      q: "A titration uses 25.00 cm³ of solution measured by pipette. State the function of the pipette and explain why it is used.",
      hint: "Distinguish the pipette from the burette.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the pipette as the apparatus used to measure a fixed volume.",
        "Explain that it transfers the measured volume into the flask."
      ],
      ans: "A pipette measures and transfers a fixed volume, such as 25.00 cm³, accurately into the conical flask.",
      why: "Using a fixed measured volume makes the titration procedure consistent."
    },
    {
      q: "Two closely agreeing titres are 21.60 cm³ and 21.80 cm³. Calculate their mean titre.",
      hint: "Add both values and divide by two.",
      steps: [
        "Add: 21.60 + 21.80 = 43.40 cm³.",
        "Divide by 2: 43.40 ÷ 2 = 21.70 cm³."
      ],
      ans: "Mean titre = 21.70 cm³.",
      why: "The mean of two values is their sum divided by two."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain the difference between the endpoint and the equivalence point in an acid-base titration.",
      hint: "One is identified by the indicator; the other concerns the reacting amounts.",
      steps: [
        "Define the endpoint in terms of the observed indicator colour change.",
        "Define the equivalence point in terms of the stoichiometric proportions in the balanced reaction.",
        "Explain why they are related but not necessarily identical."
      ],
      ans: "The endpoint is the observed indicator colour change. The equivalence point is where the acid and base have reacted in the stoichiometric proportions required by the balanced equation. A suitable indicator helps the observed endpoint approximate the equivalence point.",
      why: "The colour change is an experimental signal, while the equivalence point is defined by the chemical amounts reacting."
    }
  ]
);

