// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SCREEN 5: MEANING OF RATE OF REACTION
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Meaning of Rate of Reaction",

  `
  <h2>Meaning of Rate of Reaction</h2>

  <h3>1. Definition of Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>rate of reaction</strong> is the change in the amount of a reactant
    used up or product formed per unit time during a chemical reaction.
  </p>

  <p>
    A reaction with a high rate produces the observable change quickly. A reaction
    with a low rate produces the change more slowly. Rate describes how quickly a
    reaction proceeds, not how much product can eventually be formed.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid produces hydrogen
    gas. If the gas is produced rapidly, the reaction has a high rate. If the same
    amount of gas takes much longer to form, the reaction has a lower average rate.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Measuring the Rate of Reaction</h3>

  <p>
    The rate can be investigated by measuring a quantity that changes as the reaction
    proceeds. The measurement chosen depends on the reaction.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Volume of gas:</strong> measure the volume of gas produced over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Mass:</strong> measure the decrease in mass when a gas escapes from the reaction vessel.</li>
    <li><strong>Concentration:</strong> measure how the concentration of a reactant or product changes with time.</li>
    <li><strong>Time taken:</strong> measure how long a reaction takes to reach a clearly defined endpoint.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a reaction producing gas, a gas syringe can measure the volume collected.
    For a reaction that releases a gas from an open vessel, a balance can measure
    the decrease in mass.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Calculating Average Rate</h3>

  <p>
    If a measurable quantity increases as a product forms, the average rate can be
    calculated using:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate = Change in quantity ÷ Time taken</strong></p>

  <p>
    For gas production:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Average rate of gas production = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</strong></p>

  <p>
    If 60 cm³ of gas is produced in 20 seconds:
  </p>

  <p>Average rate = 60 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p>
    The unit depends on the quantity measured. Gas volume divided by time may be
    expressed in cm³/s. Mass divided by time may be expressed in g/s.
    Concentration divided by time may be expressed in mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Average Rate and Instantaneous Rate</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Average rate</strong> describes the change over a stated time interval.
    It is calculated by dividing the total change in the measured quantity by the
    time interval.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Instantaneous rate</strong> describes the rate at a particular moment.
    On a graph of quantity against time, it is represented by the gradient of the
    tangent to the curve at that moment.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rate often decreases as a reaction proceeds because reactants are used up.
    In many reactions, there are fewer effective collisions per second later in the
    reaction. Eventually, the measured quantity may stop changing because the reaction
    has finished or because the limiting reactant has been consumed.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating average gas-production rate</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction produces 90 cm³ of hydrogen gas in 30 seconds. Calculate its average
    rate of gas production.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>

  <p>Average rate = Volume of gas produced ÷ Time</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 90 cm³ ÷ 30 s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>

  <p>Average rate = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of gas production is 3 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing average rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces 40 cm³ of gas in 10 seconds. Reaction B produces 72 cm³
    in 24 seconds. Calculate both average rates and identify which reaction has
    the higher average rate.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction A.</p>

  <p>Rate A = 40 cm³ ÷ 10 s = 4 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate for reaction B.</p>

  <p>Rate B = 72 cm³ ÷ 24 s = 3 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the results.</p>

  <p>
    Reaction A produces an average of 4 cm³ each second, while reaction B produces
    an average of 3 cm³ each second.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Reaction A has the higher average rate by 1 cm³/s.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating a rate from mass loss</strong></p>

  <p>
    A reaction vessel has a mass of 150.0 g before a reaction begins and 147.6 g
    after 40 seconds. A gas escapes during the reaction. Calculate the average
    rate of mass loss.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the decrease in mass.</p>

  <p>Mass lost = Initial mass − Final mass</p>

  <p>= 150.0 g − 147.6 g = 2.4 g</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Divide the mass lost by the time.</p>

  <p>Average rate of mass loss = 2.4 g ÷ 40 s</p>

  <p>= 0.06 g/s</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate of mass loss is 0.06 g/s.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>A faster reaction does not necessarily produce a greater final amount of product.</li>
    <li>Always state the quantity measured and the time unit when reporting a rate.</li>
    <li>For average rate, divide the change in the measured quantity by the elapsed time.</li>
    <li>Mass loss can indicate that a gas has escaped, but it is not a direct measurement of gas volume.</li>
    <li>A rate calculated over an entire interval is an average rate, not necessarily the rate at every moment.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define the rate of reaction and explain what is meant by a high rate.",
      hint: "Think about the amount of reactant consumed or product formed in a given time.",
      steps: [
        "Identify a quantity that changes during a reaction.",
        "Relate its change to elapsed time.",
        "Explain what a high rate means."
      ],
      ans: "Rate of reaction is the change in the amount of a reactant used up or product formed per unit time. A high rate means the measured chemical change occurs quickly.",
      why: "Rate describes how quickly a reaction proceeds, rather than the total amount of product that can form."
    },
    {
      q: "A reaction produces 126 cm³ of gas in 42 seconds. Calculate the average rate of gas production and give its unit.",
      hint: "Divide the total gas volume by the time taken.",
      steps: [
        "Write: Average rate = Volume produced ÷ Time.",
        "Substitute: 126 cm³ ÷ 42 s.",
        "Calculate and include the correct unit."
      ],
      ans: "Average rate = 126 cm³ ÷ 42 s = 3 cm³/s.",
      why: "Dividing volume by time gives the average volume of gas produced each second."
    },
    {
      q: "Reaction A loses 3.6 g of mass in 60 seconds. Reaction B loses 2.8 g in 35 seconds. Calculate both average rates of mass loss and identify the faster rate of mass loss.",
      hint: "For each reaction, divide its mass loss by its own time.",
      steps: [
        "Rate A = 3.6 g ÷ 60 s.",
        "Rate B = 2.8 g ÷ 35 s.",
        "Compare the answers in g/s."
      ],
      ans: "Rate A = 0.06 g/s. Rate B = 0.08 g/s. Reaction B has the higher average rate of mass loss.",
      why: "Reaction B loses more mass per second on average, even though its total mass loss is smaller."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain two methods for measuring the rate of a reaction that produces a gas, and state what is measured in each method.",
      hint: "Consider collecting the gas and allowing it to escape from a vessel on a balance.",
      steps: [
        "Describe how a gas syringe can be used.",
        "State what quantity is recorded with a gas syringe.",
        "Describe how a balance can be used when gas escapes.",
        "State what quantity is recorded with the balance."
      ],
      ans: "A gas syringe can measure the volume of gas collected at regular time intervals. Alternatively, a reaction vessel on a balance can be monitored as gas escapes; the decrease in mass is recorded at regular intervals.",
      why: "Both methods track a changing quantity over time, allowing an average rate to be calculated."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner calculates a reaction rate by dividing the final gas volume by the initial gas volume. Explain why this method is incorrect and give the correct method.",
      hint: "Rate requires a change in quantity and a time interval.",
      steps: [
        "Recall the definition of rate.",
        "Identify what is missing from the learner's calculation.",
        "Write the correct formula for average gas-production rate."
      ],
      ans: "The method is incorrect because it does not divide a change in gas volume by a time interval. Average gas-production rate = Change in gas volume ÷ Time taken.",
      why: "A rate measures how much a quantity changes per unit time; a ratio of two volumes alone does not have the correct meaning or unit."
    },
    {
      q: "A reaction produces 20 cm³ of gas in the first 5 seconds and a further 10 cm³ in the next 5 seconds. Calculate the average rate in each interval and explain what the results suggest.",
      hint: "Calculate each interval separately using the gas produced during that interval.",
      steps: [
        "First interval rate = 20 cm³ ÷ 5 s.",
        "Second interval rate = 10 cm³ ÷ 5 s.",
        "Compare the two rates.",
        "Explain what the difference may indicate."
      ],
      ans: "First interval rate = 4 cm³/s. Second interval rate = 2 cm³/s. The average rate decreased in the second interval.",
      why: "The reaction may be slowing as reactants are consumed and effective collisions become less frequent."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SCREEN 6: EFFECT OF TEMPERATURE ON RATE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Effect of Temperature on Rate",

  `
  <h2>Effect of Temperature on Rate</h2>

  <h3>1. How Temperature Affects Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing the temperature usually increases the rate of a chemical reaction.
    Decreasing the temperature usually reduces the rate, provided the reaction
    conditions remain comparable.
  </p>

  <p>
    Temperature affects the average kinetic energy of particles. At a higher
    temperature, reacting particles move faster on average and have greater kinetic
    energy. This changes both how frequently particles collide and how many collisions
    have enough energy to lead to a reaction.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Collision Frequency</h3>

  <p>
    When particles move faster, they can collide more frequently under suitable
    conditions. This can increase the number of opportunities for a reaction to occur.
  </p>

  <p>
    However, the increased collision frequency is not the only explanation for the
    effect of temperature. The more important factor is the increase in the proportion
    of collisions with sufficient energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Energy and Successful Collisions</h3>

  <p>
    Not every collision between reacting particles produces a reaction. For a collision
    to be successful, the particles must have sufficient energy to overcome the
    activation energy and must interact in a suitable way.
  </p>

  <p>
    When temperature increases, the distribution of particle energies changes.
    A greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or greater than the
    activation energy. Consequently, more collisions can result in a reaction per
    second, increasing the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Example: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce magnesium chloride
    and hydrogen gas:
  </p>

  <p>Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    If the acid is warmer, the reaction will generally produce hydrogen faster.
    A fair comparison requires the same acid concentration and volume, the same
    magnesium mass and exposed surface area, and the same measuring method.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Investigating Temperature Fairly</h3>

  <p>
    To investigate temperature, use the same quantities and concentrations of
    reactants while changing only the starting temperature.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>Measure a fixed volume of acid at a known concentration.</li>
    <li>Bring it to the selected starting temperature.</li>
    <li>Add the same mass and form of magnesium.</li>
    <li>Measure the gas volume at regular time intervals.</li>
    <li>Repeat at another temperature while keeping the other conditions the same.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Compare the gas volume produced in the same time interval, or calculate the
    average rate for each experiment. Use suitable eye protection and follow
    laboratory safety procedures when handling acids and flammable hydrogen.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the effect of temperature</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why magnesium generally reacts faster with dilute hydrochloric acid
    at 40°C than at 20°C.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the particle change. At 40°C, particles have
  greater average kinetic energy than at 20°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider collisions. The particles move faster and may
  collide more frequently.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Consider activation energy. A greater proportion of
  collisions have enough energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> More successful collisions occur per second, so
  the reaction is generally faster at 40°C.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Comparing measured rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    At 20°C, a reaction produces 24 cm³ of gas in 30 seconds. At 40°C, it produces
    54 cm³ in the same time. Calculate both average rates and compare them.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate at 20°C.</p>

  <p>Rate = 24 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 0.8 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate at 40°C.</p>

  <p>Rate = 54 cm³ ÷ 30 s = 1.8 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the rates.</p>

  <p>Difference = 1.8 − 0.8 = 1.0 cm³/s</p>

  <p>Rate ratio = 1.8 ÷ 0.8 = 2.25</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The average rate at 40°C is 2.25 times the
  average rate at 20°C for these measurements.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Designing a fair test</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner compares the rate of magnesium reacting with acid at 20°C and 40°C.
    In the second experiment, the learner also uses more concentrated acid and
    smaller magnesium pieces. Explain why the results cannot establish the effect
    of temperature alone.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the intended independent variable: temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the additional changes: acid concentration and
  magnesium surface area.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Explain the problem. Both changes can affect the rate
  independently of temperature.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The test is not fair. Keep acid concentration and
  magnesium surface area constant, changing only the temperature.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Higher temperature generally increases reaction rate; it does not automatically increase the final amount of product if the reactants and limiting reactant remain unchanged.</li>
    <li>The effect is not explained only by more frequent collisions. The greater proportion of particles with sufficient energy is crucial.</li>
    <li>Temperature comparisons require other rate-affecting variables to be controlled.</li>
    <li>A reaction being twice as fast in one experiment does not establish a universal rule that every 20°C increase doubles every reaction rate.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Explain why increasing temperature generally increases the rate of a chemical reaction.",
      hint: "Discuss kinetic energy, activation energy and successful collisions.",
      steps: [
        "Explain how particle kinetic energy changes with temperature.",
        "Explain how particle movement and collisions are affected.",
        "Explain how the proportion of particles able to overcome activation energy changes.",
        "Connect these changes to reaction rate."
      ],
      ans: "Increasing temperature raises the average kinetic energy of particles. They move faster, and a greater proportion have enough energy to overcome the activation-energy barrier. More successful collisions occur per second, so the reaction rate generally increases.",
      why: "The increase in the proportion of sufficiently energetic collisions is a key reason temperature strongly affects reaction rate."
    },
    {
      q: "At 25°C, a reaction produces 36 cm³ of gas in 45 seconds. At 45°C, it produces 72 cm³ in the same time. Calculate both average rates and compare them.",
      hint: "Divide each gas volume by 45 seconds, then compare the answers.",
      steps: [
        "Rate at 25°C = 36 cm³ ÷ 45 s.",
        "Rate at 45°C = 72 cm³ ÷ 45 s.",
        "Compare the calculated rates and their ratio."
      ],
      ans: "Rate at 25°C = 0.8 cm³/s. Rate at 45°C = 1.6 cm³/s. The second rate is twice the first.",
      why: "The reaction produced twice as much gas in the same time at 45°C, so its average gas-production rate was twice as high in these measurements."
    },
    {
      q: "Describe a fair experiment to investigate how temperature affects the rate of magnesium reacting with dilute hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "Change temperature while keeping the other important conditions constant.",
      steps: [
        "Choose and measure a fixed volume and concentration of acid.",
        "Set the acid to a known starting temperature.",
        "Add the same mass and form of magnesium.",
        "Measure the hydrogen volume at regular intervals.",
        "Repeat at another temperature while keeping the other variables constant.",
        "Compare the rates."
      ],
      ans: "Use the same volume and concentration of acid and the same mass and surface area of magnesium. Set the acid to a chosen temperature, add the magnesium, and measure hydrogen volume at regular intervals. Repeat at another temperature under otherwise identical conditions and compare the average rates.",
      why: "Changing only temperature allows differences in rate to be attributed more confidently to temperature."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why a greater proportion of particles can react successfully at a higher temperature.",
      hint: "Relate particle energy to the activation-energy threshold.",
      steps: [
        "Recall that particles have a range of kinetic energies.",
        "Explain what happens to the energy distribution when temperature increases.",
        "Compare the proportion of particles above the activation energy.",
        "Connect the change to successful collisions."
      ],
      ans: "Particles have a range of energies. At a higher temperature, the distribution shifts so that a greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy. Therefore, more collisions can lead to successful reactions.",
      why: "The reaction rate depends on the number of successful collisions per unit time, not merely on whether particles collide."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner heats the acid, increases its concentration and uses powdered magnesium instead of a ribbon. Explain why the learner cannot identify the effect of temperature alone.",
      hint: "Identify each variable that can affect the rate.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the variable being investigated.",
        "Identify the other two changed variables.",
        "Explain how each extra change can affect the rate.",
        "State how the experiment should be improved."
      ],
      ans: "Temperature, acid concentration and magnesium surface area all affect reaction rate. Because three variables changed, the results cannot show the effect of temperature alone. Keep concentration and magnesium surface area constant and change only temperature.",
      why: "A fair test controls other factors that could explain the observed change in rate."
    },
    {
      q: "A reaction takes 80 seconds at a lower temperature and 40 seconds at a higher temperature to reach the same endpoint. Calculate the ratio of their average rates and explain your reasoning.",
      hint: "For the same amount of change, average rate is inversely proportional to the time taken.",
      steps: [
        "Let the same amount of reaction change be represented by Q.",
        "Write the lower-temperature rate as Q ÷ 80.",
        "Write the higher-temperature rate as Q ÷ 40.",
        "Divide the higher-temperature rate by the lower-temperature rate."
      ],
      ans: "Rate ratio = (Q ÷ 40) ÷ (Q ÷ 80) = 80 ÷ 40 = 2. The average rate at the higher temperature is twice the lower-temperature rate.",
      why: "When the same amount of change is measured, completing it in half the time corresponds to twice the average rate."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// SCREEN 7: EFFECT OF CONCENTRATION ON RATE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Effect of Concentration on Rate",

  `
  <h2>Effect of Concentration on Rate</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Concentration</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Concentration</strong> describes how much dissolved substance is present
    in a given volume of solution. A more concentrated solution contains more
    dissolved particles per unit volume than a more dilute solution of the same
    substance.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concentration is commonly expressed in mol dm⁻³. For example, a solution with
    a concentration of 2.0 mol dm⁻³ contains twice as many moles of dissolved
    solute per cubic decimetre as a solution with a concentration of 1.0 mol dm⁻³.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concentration is not the same as total amount. A large volume of dilute solution
    can contain more total solute than a small volume of concentrated solution.
    Concentration compares amount with volume.
  </p>

  <h3>2. How Concentration Affects Reaction Rate</h3>

  <p>
    Increasing the concentration of a reactant in solution usually increases the
    rate of reaction, provided other relevant conditions remain unchanged.
  </p>

  <p>
    A more concentrated solution has more reactant particles in the same volume.
    The particles therefore have more opportunities to collide with other reacting
    particles. This generally increases the number of effective collisions per
    second and raises the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Diluting a solution reduces the number of reactant particles per unit volume.
    Collisions between reacting particles become less frequent, so the rate
    generally decreases.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Example: Magnesium and Hydrochloric Acid</h3>

  <p>
    Magnesium reacts with hydrochloric acid to form magnesium chloride and hydrogen:
  </p>

  <p>Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)</p>

  <p>
    If the concentration of hydrochloric acid increases while the magnesium mass,
    surface area, temperature and acid volume are controlled appropriately, hydrogen
    will generally be produced at a faster rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fair comparison should keep the other conditions the same. The acid volume
    and concentration also determine the total amount of acid available, so the
    final quantity of hydrogen may differ if the amount of limiting reactant differs.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Dilution Calculations</h3>

  <p>
    When a solution is diluted by adding water, the amount of dissolved solute
    remains constant, but the solution volume increases. The concentration decreases.
  </p>

  <p>
    For dilution calculations:
  </p>

  <p><strong>C₁V₁ = C₂V₂</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>C₁:</strong> initial concentration.</li>
    <li><strong>V₁:</strong> initial volume of solution taken.</li>
    <li><strong>C₂:</strong> final concentration after dilution.</li>
    <li><strong>V₂:</strong> final total solution volume.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Use consistent volume units on both sides. This equation calculates concentration
    after dilution; it does not directly calculate the reaction rate.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Explaining the concentration effect</strong></p>

  <p>
    Explain why magnesium generally reacts faster with 2.0 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric
    acid than with 0.5 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid at the same temperature.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Compare the concentrations. The 2.0 mol dm⁻³ solution
  contains more acid particles per unit volume.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider collisions. More acid particles are available
  to collide with the magnesium surface in a given time.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Connect to rate. More effective collisions occur per
  second, so the reaction is generally faster.</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The more concentrated acid generally reacts faster
  when other relevant conditions are controlled.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating concentration after dilution</strong></p>

  <p>
    A learner takes 25 cm³ of hydrochloric acid at 2.0 mol dm⁻³ and adds water
    until the total volume is 100 cm³. Calculate the final concentration.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the dilution formula.</p>

  <p>C₁V₁ = C₂V₂</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>

  <p>2.0 × 25 = C₂ × 100</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Rearrange to find C₂.</p>

  <p>C₂ = (2.0 × 25) ÷ 100</p>

  <p>C₂ = 50 ÷ 100 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The final acid concentration is 0.50 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Comparing measured reaction rates</strong></p>

  <p>
    With 1.0 mol dm⁻³ acid, a reaction produces 30 cm³ of gas in 20 seconds.
    With 2.0 mol dm⁻³ acid, it produces 48 cm³ in the same time. Calculate both
    average rates and compare them.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Calculate the rate at 1.0 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p>Rate = 30 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 1.5 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Calculate the rate at 2.0 mol dm⁻³.</p>

  <p>Rate = 48 cm³ ÷ 20 s = 2.4 cm³/s</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare the rates.</p>

  <p>Rate difference = 2.4 − 1.5 = 0.9 cm³/s</p>

  <p>Rate ratio = 2.4 ÷ 1.5 = 1.6</p>

  <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The measured average rate at 2.0 mol dm⁻³ is
  1.6 times the rate at 1.0 mol dm⁻³. These results do not establish that rate is
  always directly proportional to concentration.</p>

  <h3>6. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Concentration is the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume, not the total amount in the container.</li>
    <li>Dilution increases solution volume while the amount of solute remains constant.</li>
    <li>A more concentrated solution generally reacts faster under comparable conditions.</li>
    <li>Doubling concentration does not necessarily double reaction rate; the relationship depends on the reaction.</li>
    <li>To investigate concentration fairly, control temperature, reactant surface area and other relevant variables.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define concentration and explain why a more concentrated solution generally reacts faster.",
      hint: "Consider the amount of dissolved substance in a fixed volume.",
      steps: [
        "Define concentration as amount of solute per unit volume.",
        "Compare the number of reactant particles in equal volumes of concentrated and dilute solutions.",
        "Connect particle availability to collision frequency and reaction rate."
      ],
      ans: "Concentration describes the amount of dissolved substance per unit volume of solution. A more concentrated solution contains more reactant particles per unit volume, so collisions between reacting particles generally occur more frequently and the rate increases.",
      why: "More reactant particles in a given volume provide more opportunities for effective collisions."
    },
    {
      q: "Calculate the final concentration when 40 cm³ of a 1.5 mol dm⁻³ solution is diluted with water to a total volume of 120 cm³.",
      hint: "Use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.",
      steps: [
        "Write C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.",
        "Substitute: 1.5 × 40 = C₂ × 120.",
        "Rearrange: C₂ = (1.5 × 40) ÷ 120.",
        "Calculate the final concentration."
      ],
      ans: "C₂ = (1.5 × 40) ÷ 120 = 60 ÷ 120 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³.",
      why: "The amount of solute stays constant during dilution while the total volume increases, reducing the concentration."
    },
    {
      q: "A reaction produces 42 cm³ of gas in 30 seconds using dilute acid and 63 cm³ in 30 seconds using more concentrated acid. Calculate both average rates and their ratio.",
      hint: "Calculate each volume divided by 30 seconds, then divide the larger rate by the smaller rate.",
      steps: [
        "Dilute-acid rate = 42 cm³ ÷ 30 s.",
        "Concentrated-acid rate = 63 cm³ ÷ 30 s.",
        "Divide the concentrated-acid rate by the dilute-acid rate."
      ],
      ans: "Dilute-acid rate = 1.4 cm³/s. Concentrated-acid rate = 2.1 cm³/s. Rate ratio = 2.1 ÷ 1.4 = 1.5.",
      why: "Under the measured conditions, the concentrated-acid reaction has 1.5 times the average gas-production rate."
    },
    {
      q: "Describe a fair experiment for investigating the effect of acid concentration on the rate of magnesium reacting with hydrochloric acid.",
      hint: "Change acid concentration only and measure a quantity that changes over time.",
      steps: [
        "Prepare acid solutions of different known concentrations.",
        "Use the same volume of each solution and maintain the same temperature.",
        "Use the same mass and exposed surface area of magnesium.",
        "Measure the volume of hydrogen at regular time intervals.",
        "Compare the rates while controlling other relevant conditions."
      ],
      ans: "React equal volumes of hydrochloric acid at different known concentrations with identical masses and surface areas of magnesium at the same temperature. Measure the hydrogen volume at regular intervals and calculate or compare the rates.",
      why: "Keeping other rate-affecting variables constant allows the effect of acid concentration to be investigated."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why doubling the concentration of a reactant does not guarantee that the reaction rate will double.",
      hint: "Distinguish a general collision explanation from an exact mathematical relationship.",
      steps: [
        "Explain how increasing concentration affects particle availability.",
        "State the general effect on collision frequency.",
        "Explain why the exact rate relationship depends on the reaction."
      ],
      ans: "Doubling concentration increases the number of reactant particles per unit volume and generally increases the rate. However, the exact relationship depends on the reaction mechanism and its rate law, so the rate does not necessarily double.",
      why: "Concentration commonly affects collision frequency, but different reactions have different quantitative relationships between concentration and rate."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner dilutes 50 cm³ of a 3.0 mol dm⁻³ solution to a total volume of 150 cm³. Calculate the final concentration and explain what happens to the amount of solute.",
      hint: "Use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ and remember that water adds volume, not solute.",
      steps: [
        "Write C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.",
        "Substitute: 3.0 × 50 = C₂ × 150.",
        "Calculate C₂ = 150 ÷ 150.",
        "State whether the amount of dissolved solute changes."
      ],
      ans: "C₂ = (3.0 × 50) ÷ 150 = 1.0 mol dm⁻³. The amount of solute remains constant during dilution, but its concentration decreases because the solution volume increases.",
      why: "Dilution spreads the same amount of solute through a larger volume."
    }
  ]
);


