add(
  "chemistry",
  "energy_rate_reaction",
  "Latent Heat of Vaporisation",

  `
  <h2>Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Vaporisation</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Vaporisation</strong> is the change of state from liquid to gas.
    It includes boiling, which occurs throughout a liquid at its boiling point,
    and evaporation, which can occur at the surface of a liquid below its boiling point.
  </p>

  <p>
    During boiling at a constant pressure, a pure liquid can absorb energy without
    its temperature increasing while it changes into gas. The supplied energy is
    used for the phase change.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Meaning of Specific Latent Heat of Vaporisation</h3>

  <p>
    The <strong>specific latent heat of vaporisation</strong> is the energy required
    to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its
    temperature.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is represented by Lv and measured in <strong>J kg⁻¹</strong>.
    Different substances have different specific latent heats of vaporisation.
  </p>

  <h3>3. The Latent Heat Formula</h3>

  <p><strong>Q = mLv</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Q:</strong> energy transferred, in joules (J).</li>
    <li><strong>m:</strong> mass vaporised, in kilograms (kg).</li>
    <li><strong>Lv:</strong> specific latent heat of vaporisation, in J kg⁻¹.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    To calculate the energy required to vaporise a mass, multiply the mass by
    the specific latent heat of vaporisation. To calculate the mass vaporised,
    divide the energy by Lv.
  </p>

  <h3>4. What Happens During Boiling?</h3>

  <p>
    As a liquid absorbs energy and reaches its boiling point, the energy supplied
    during boiling is used to separate particles sufficiently to form a gas.
    The temperature remains constant while the liquid and gas coexist during
    the phase change of a pure substance at constant pressure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once all the liquid has vaporised, further heating can increase the temperature
    of the gas. If a gas condenses back into a liquid, energy is released to the
    surroundings.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Vaporisation Compared with Fusion</h3>

  <p>
    Fusion is the change from solid to liquid and uses Q = mLf. Vaporisation is
    the change from liquid to gas and uses Q = mLv.
  </p>

  <p>
    Both are phase changes. During each change, the temperature of a pure substance
    remains constant under the stated transition conditions, while energy is
    transferred to change the state.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Calculating energy to vaporise water</strong></p>

  <p>
    Calculate the energy required to vaporise 0.5 kg of water at its boiling point.
    Use Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = mLv</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>Q = 0.5 × 2,260,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 1,130,000 J = 1,130 kJ</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 1,130,000 J is required to vaporise the water.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating the mass vaporised</strong></p>

  <p>
    A quantity of water at its boiling point absorbs 678,000 J during vaporisation.
    Calculate the mass vaporised. Use Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange Q = mLv.</p>
  <p>m = Q ÷ Lv</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute.</p>
  <p>m = 678,000 ÷ 2,260,000</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>m = 0.30 kg</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The mass vaporised is 0.30 kg.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Calculating specific latent heat of vaporisation</strong></p>

  <p>
    A 0.2 kg liquid absorbs 460,000 J to change completely into gas at its boiling
    point. Calculate its specific latent heat of vaporisation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Start with Q = mLv.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Rearrange.</p>
  <p>Lv = Q ÷ m</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Substitute and calculate.</p>
  <p>Lv = 460,000 ÷ 0.2 = 2,300,000 J kg⁻¹</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The specific latent heat of vaporisation is
  2,300,000 J kg⁻¹.</p>

  <h3>7. Common Misunderstandings</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Vaporisation means liquid to gas; boiling is one form of vaporisation.</li>
    <li>Use Q = mLv for energy transferred during vaporisation at the boiling point.</li>
    <li>Specific latent heat is measured in J kg⁻¹, not J kg⁻¹ °C⁻¹.</li>
    <li>During boiling, energy is absorbed while the temperature of a pure substance remains constant under constant-pressure conditions.</li>
    <li>Evaporation can occur below the boiling point and does not require the entire liquid to reach its boiling point.</li>
  </ul>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Define specific latent heat of vaporisation and state its SI unit.",
      hint: "Describe the energy needed to change a unit mass from liquid to gas without a temperature change.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the change from liquid to gas.",
        "State the mass used in the definition.",
        "State the temperature condition.",
        "Give the SI unit."
      ],
      ans: "Specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy required to change 1 kg of a liquid into gas at its boiling point without changing its temperature. Its SI unit is J kg⁻¹.",
      why: "It measures the energy required per kilogram for vaporisation, rather than for a temperature increase."
    },
    {
      q: "Calculate the energy required to vaporise 0.8 kg of water at its boiling point. Use Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.",
      hint: "Use Q = mLv.",
      steps: [
        "Write Q = mLv.",
        "Substitute: Q = 0.8 × 2,260,000.",
        "Calculate the energy in joules.",
        "Convert to kilojoules if required."
      ],
      ans: "Q = 0.8 × 2,260,000 = 1,808,000 J = 1,808 kJ.",
      why: "The energy required for vaporisation is the mass multiplied by the specific latent heat."
    },
    {
      q: "Water at its boiling point absorbs 565,000 J during vaporisation. Calculate the mass vaporised using Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.",
      hint: "Rearrange Q = mLv to find mass.",
      steps: [
        "Write m = Q ÷ Lv.",
        "Substitute: m = 565,000 ÷ 2,260,000.",
        "Calculate the mass in kilograms."
      ],
      ans: "m = 565,000 ÷ 2,260,000 = 0.25 kg.",
      why: "Dividing the energy absorbed by the energy needed to vaporise one kilogram gives the mass vaporised."
    },
    {
      q: "A 0.4 kg liquid absorbs 920,000 J as it changes completely into gas at its boiling point. Calculate its specific latent heat of vaporisation.",
      hint: "Divide the energy by the mass.",
      steps: [
        "Write Q = mLv.",
        "Rearrange: Lv = Q ÷ m.",
        "Substitute: Lv = 920,000 ÷ 0.4.",
        "Calculate and include the unit."
      ],
      ans: "Lv = 920,000 ÷ 0.4 = 2,300,000 J kg⁻¹.",
      why: "Specific latent heat of vaporisation is the energy transferred per kilogram during the liquid-to-gas phase change."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain why the temperature of boiling water remains constant while it changes into steam, even though heating continues.",
      hint: "Explain the purpose of the supplied energy during the phase change.",
      steps: [
        "State that energy continues to enter the water.",
        "Explain what happens to the liquid particles during vaporisation.",
        "Explain why the temperature remains constant during the phase change.",
        "State what happens after all the liquid has vaporised."
      ],
      ans: "During boiling, the supplied energy is used to separate particles sufficiently for the liquid to become gas rather than raising the temperature. The temperature remains constant while the liquid changes state at constant pressure. After all the liquid has vaporised, further heating can raise the steam's temperature.",
      why: "The energy is being used for the change of state rather than increasing the average kinetic energy of the particles."
    },
    {
      q: "A learner uses Q = mcΔT to calculate the energy required to turn 0.5 kg of water at 100°C into steam at 100°C. Explain why the formula is unsuitable and calculate the required energy using Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹.",
      hint: "The initial and final temperatures are the same, but the state changes.",
      steps: [
        "Calculate the temperature difference.",
        "Explain why Q = mcΔT does not represent the phase-change energy.",
        "Use Q = mLv.",
        "Substitute m = 0.5 kg and Lv = 2,260,000 J kg⁻¹."
      ],
      ans: "The temperature difference is 0°C, so Q = mcΔT does not calculate the energy required for the state change. Use Q = mLv = 0.5 × 2,260,000 = 1,130,000 J.",
      why: "The energy is used for vaporisation at constant temperature, so the latent heat formula is required."
    }
  ]
);

