// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 11. SIMPLE CELLS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "electrochemistry",
  "Simple Cells",

  `
  <h2>Simple Cells</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Simple Cell</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>simple cell</strong> is a device that converts chemical energy
    into electrical energy through chemical reactions.
  </p>

  <p>
    A basic cell consists of two different electrodes placed in an electrolyte.
    The electrodes are connected externally by a conducting wire. When the
    circuit is complete, chemical reactions can cause electrons to flow
    through the external wire.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Main Components</h3>

  <p><strong>Electrodes:</strong> Conducting materials through which electrons
  enter or leave the cell. Different metals, such as zinc and copper, may be
  used.</p>

  <p><strong>Electrolyte:</strong> A liquid or solution containing mobile ions
  that carry charge inside the cell.</p>

  <p><strong>External circuit:</strong> The wire and any connected device
  through which electrons flow outside the cell.</p>

  <h3>3. How a Simple Cell Works</h3>

  <p>
    The two electrodes have different tendencies to undergo chemical
    reactions. When suitable electrodes and an electrolyte are connected
    in a complete circuit, one electrode may release electrons while a
    reaction at the other electrode accepts electrons.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a typical zinc-copper cell, zinc is oxidised and releases electrons:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻</strong></p>

  <p>
    The electrons travel through the external wire from the zinc electrode
    towards the copper electrode. At the copper electrode, copper ions may
    accept electrons:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)</strong></p>

  <p>
    These half-equations illustrate a zinc-copper cell containing suitable
    zinc and copper ion solutions. The actual reactions depend on the
    materials and electrolyte used.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Electron Flow and Conventional Current</h3>

  <p>
    In the external circuit of the zinc-copper cell described above,
    electrons flow from zinc to copper. Conventional current is defined
    in the opposite direction, from the positive terminal towards the
    negative terminal through the external circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Do not confuse electron flow with conventional current: they point in
    opposite directions in a metallic wire.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Identifying the components</strong></p>

  <p>
    A cell contains zinc and copper electrodes immersed in suitable
    electrolyte solutions. Identify three essential components.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the conducting materials.</p>
  <p>The zinc and copper strips are the two electrodes.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the ion-containing medium.</p>
  <p>The electrolyte solutions provide mobile ions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Identify the external path.</p>
  <p>A conducting wire connects the electrodes and allows electrons to flow.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Two electrodes, an electrolyte, and an external
  conducting circuit.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Writing the oxidation equation</strong></p>

  <p>
    Zinc atoms form zinc ions by losing two electrons. Write the half-equation.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the change in the chemical species.</p>
  <p>Zn → Zn²⁺</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Balance the electrical charge.</p>
  <p>The zinc ion has a charge of +2, so two electrons must appear on the
  product side.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Determining electron direction</strong></p>

  <p>
    In the zinc-copper cell described in these notes, which electrode supplies
    electrons to the external wire?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the oxidation reaction.</p>
  <p>Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify where electrons are released.</p>
  <p>Zinc releases the electrons.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Follow their path.</p>
  <p>The electrons travel through the external wire towards the copper
  electrode.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The zinc electrode supplies electrons to the
  external circuit.</p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "A simple cell contains two electrodes, an electrolyte and a wire. State the function of each of these components.",
      hint: "Consider where electrons travel and where ions move.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the two conducting electrodes.",
        "Explain the role of the electrolyte in carrying charge through mobile ions.",
        "Explain how the wire provides an external path for electron flow."
      ],
      ans: "The electrodes provide surfaces for chemical reactions and electron transfer. The electrolyte conducts charge through mobile ions. The wire connects the electrodes and allows electrons to flow through the external circuit.",
      why: "A working cell requires charge transfer both inside the cell through ions and outside it through the conducting circuit."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the half-equation for zinc atoms forming zinc ions.",
      hint: "Zinc forms Zn²⁺, so account for a charge of +2.",
      steps: [
        "Write Zn → Zn²⁺.",
        "The right side has a charge of +2.",
        "Add two electrons to the right side to balance the charge."
      ],
      ans: "Zn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻",
      why: "Zinc loses two electrons. Loss of electrons is oxidation."
    },
    {
      q: "Write the half-equation for copper(II) ions gaining electrons to form copper metal.",
      hint: "Cu²⁺ must gain enough electrons to become neutral.",
      steps: [
        "Start with Cu²⁺ → Cu.",
        "The left side has a charge of +2 while the right side is neutral.",
        "Add two electrons to the left side."
      ],
      ans: "Cu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)",
      why: "Copper(II) ions gain two electrons. Gain of electrons is reduction."
    },
    {
      q: "In the zinc-copper cell described in the notes, state the direction of electron flow in the external wire and explain why.",
      hint: "Find the electrode at which electrons are released.",
      steps: [
        "Zinc undergoes oxidation.",
        "Oxidation releases electrons.",
        "The released electrons travel through the wire towards the copper electrode."
      ],
      ans: "Electrons flow from zinc to copper because zinc releases electrons during oxidation.",
      why: "Electron flow follows the external conducting path from the electrode releasing electrons towards the electrode where reduction occurs."
    },
    {
      q: "Explain the difference between electron flow and conventional current in the external wire of a zinc-copper cell.",
      hint: "Conventional current is defined in the direction positive charge would move.",
      steps: [
        "State the direction of electron movement.",
        "State the defined direction of conventional current.",
        "Compare the two directions."
      ],
      ans: "Electrons flow from zinc to copper, while conventional current flows in the opposite direction, from copper towards zinc through the external circuit.",
      why: "Electrons are negatively charged, whereas conventional current is defined using the direction of positive charge movement."
    },
    {
      q: "A student connects zinc and copper electrodes to a voltmeter but leaves the circuit otherwise open. Explain what the voltmeter can indicate and why a continuous current may not flow through an external load.",
      hint: "Distinguish a potential difference from a complete conducting path.",
      steps: [
        "Recognise that suitable electrode reactions can establish a potential difference.",
        "Consider whether the external circuit is complete.",
        "Explain the consequence of the open circuit for continuous current."
      ],
      ans: "The voltmeter may indicate a potential difference between the electrodes. However, an open circuit prevents a continuous current from flowing through an external load.",
      why: "A potential difference can exist even when the circuit is open, but sustained current requires a complete conducting path."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 12. CHEMICAL CELL VOLTAGE
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "electrochemistry",
  "Chemical Cell Voltage",

  `
  <h2>Chemical Cell Voltage</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of Cell Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Cell voltage</strong> is the electrical potential difference
    between the terminals of a cell. It describes the energy transferred
    per unit charge between two points.
  </p>

  <p>
    Voltage is measured in volts (V). A volt is equivalent to one joule
    of energy transferred per coulomb of charge.
  </p>

  <p><strong>1 V = 1 J/C</strong></p>

  <h3>2. Formula Linking Voltage, Energy and Charge</h3>

  <p>
    When a quantity of charge transfers an amount of energy, the average
    energy transferred per unit charge is:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = E / Q</strong></p>

  <p>Where:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>V = potential difference in volts (V)</li>
    <li>E = energy transferred in joules (J)</li>
    <li>Q = charge transferred in coulombs (C)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Rearranging the formula gives:
  </p>

  <p><strong>E = V × Q</strong></p>
  <p><strong>Q = E / V</strong></p>

  <h3>3. Worked Example: Calculating Voltage</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 12 J of energy when 3 C of charge passes through it.
    Calculate its voltage.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the formula.</p>
  <p>V = E / Q</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>V = 12 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>V = 4 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4 V</p>

  <h3>4. Worked Example: Calculating Energy</h3>

  <p>
    A 2.0 V cell transfers 5 C of charge. Calculate the energy transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Use E = V × Q.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>E = 2.0 × 5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>E = 10 J</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 10 J</p>

  <h3>5. Worked Example: Calculating Charge</h3>

  <p>
    A cell transfers 18 J of energy at a potential difference of 3 V.
    Calculate the charge transferred.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Rearrange the formula.</p>
  <p>Q = E / V</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>Q = 18 / 3</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>Q = 6 C</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 6 C</p>

  <h3>6. Combining Cell Voltages</h3>

  <p>
    For ideal cells connected in series in the same direction, their
    voltages add. If cells are connected in opposition, their voltages
    subtract, assuming the opposing voltages are unequal.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, two 1.5 V cells connected in series in the same direction
    provide a total voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    Two cells of 1.5 V and 1.0 V connected in opposition provide a net
    voltage of:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 − 1.0 = 0.5 V</strong></p>

  <p>
    These calculations describe ideal voltage combinations. The terminal
    voltage of a real cell under load can differ because of internal
    resistance and other losses.
  </p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "A cell transfers 12 J of energy when 3 C of charge passes through it. Calculate the voltage.",
      hint: "Use V = E / Q.",
      steps: [
        "Write V = E / Q.",
        "Substitute E = 12 J and Q = 3 C.",
        "Calculate 12 / 3."
      ],
      ans: "V = 12 / 3 = 4 V",
      why: "Voltage is energy transferred per unit charge."
    },
    {
      q: "A cell operates at 2.0 V while transferring 5 C of charge. Calculate the energy transferred.",
      hint: "Rearrange V = E / Q to make E the subject.",
      steps: [
        "Use E = V × Q.",
        "Substitute V = 2.0 V and Q = 5 C.",
        "Multiply 2.0 by 5."
      ],
      ans: "E = 2.0 × 5 = 10 J",
      why: "Energy transferred equals potential difference multiplied by charge."
    },
    {
      q: "A cell transfers 18 J of energy at a voltage of 3 V. Calculate the charge transferred.",
      hint: "Use Q = E / V.",
      steps: [
        "Write Q = E / V.",
        "Substitute E = 18 J and V = 3 V.",
        "Calculate 18 / 3."
      ],
      ans: "Q = 18 / 3 = 6 C",
      why: "Dividing the energy transferred by the voltage gives the charge transferred."
    },
    {
      q: "A cell transfers 24 J of energy when 4 C of charge passes through it. Calculate its voltage and state the meaning of your answer.",
      hint: "First calculate energy per coulomb.",
      steps: [
        "Use V = E / Q.",
        "Substitute V = 24 / 4.",
        "Calculate the voltage.",
        "Interpret the result in joules per coulomb."
      ],
      ans: "V = 24 / 4 = 6 V. The cell transfers 6 J of energy per coulomb of charge.",
      why: "One volt is one joule per coulomb."
    },
    {
      q: "Two ideal 1.5 V cells are connected in series in the same direction. Calculate the total voltage and explain the operation used.",
      hint: "Voltages of cells connected in series in the same direction add.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the voltage of each cell as 1.5 V.",
        "Add the two voltages.",
        "State the total voltage."
      ],
      ans: "V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V",
      why: "In an ideal series arrangement with the same orientation, the potential differences add."
    },
    {
      q: "Two ideal cells of 1.5 V and 1.0 V are connected in opposition. Calculate the net voltage and state which cell determines the direction of the net potential difference.",
      hint: "Subtract the smaller opposing voltage from the larger one.",
      steps: [
        "Write the opposing voltages as 1.5 V and 1.0 V.",
        "Calculate 1.5 − 1.0.",
        "Compare the two values to identify the larger voltage."
      ],
      ans: "Net voltage = 1.5 − 1.0 = 0.5 V. The 1.5 V cell determines the direction of the net potential difference.",
      why: "Opposing potential differences subtract. The larger one determines the net direction when the voltages are unequal."
    }
  ]
);


// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 13. BATTERIES
// ------------------------------------------------------------

add(
  "chemistry",
  "electrochemistry",
  "Batteries",

  `
  <h2>Batteries</h2>

  <h3>1. Meaning of a Battery</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>battery</strong> is a source of electrical energy made from
    one or more electrochemical cells. A battery converts stored chemical
    energy into electrical energy while it operates as a source.
  </p>

  <p>
    In everyday language, a single electrochemical cell is often called
    a battery, but technically a battery can contain several cells.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Primary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>primary battery</strong> uses chemical reactions that are
    generally not designed to be reversed by ordinary charging. Once its
    useful reactants are depleted, the battery is replaced.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include many disposable alkaline cells used in clocks,
    remote controls and simple portable devices.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Secondary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    A <strong>secondary battery</strong> is designed to be recharged.
    During discharge, chemical reactions provide electrical energy.
    When a suitable external power source drives the reverse reactions,
    energy is stored again in chemical form.
  </p>

  <p>
    Examples include rechargeable lithium-ion batteries used in phones
    and laptops, and lead-acid batteries used in many vehicles.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Comparing Primary and Secondary Batteries</h3>

  <p>
    The key distinction is whether the cell chemistry is designed to be
    restored by charging. Primary batteries are generally single-use;
    secondary batteries are designed for repeated charge-discharge cycles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rechargeable does not mean that a battery lasts forever. Repeated
    cycling, heat, age and unsuitable charging can reduce its capacity.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Batteries Connected in Series</h3>

  <p>
    When compatible cells are connected in series in the same direction,
    their voltages add. This arrangement is used when a device requires
    a higher voltage than one cell can provide.
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, three ideal 1.5 V cells connected in series provide:
  </p>

  <p><strong>V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.5 V</strong></p>

  <h3>6. Batteries Connected in Parallel</h3>

  <p>
    When compatible cells with the same voltage are connected in parallel
    with matching polarity, the voltage remains approximately the same
    as that of one cell. The arrangement may provide greater capacity
    or longer operating time, depending on the cells and circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Cells should not be connected in parallel indiscriminately. Their
    chemistry, voltage, condition and protection requirements must be
    compatible.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Worked Examples</h3>

  <p><strong>Example 1: Classifying a battery</strong></p>

  <p>
    A device uses a cell designed to be recharged after its energy has
    been used. Classify the cell.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify whether charging is intended to
  restore its chemical state.</p>
  <p>Charging is designed to reverse suitable reactions.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Match the description to the battery type.</p>
  <p>A battery designed for repeated recharging is secondary.</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A secondary battery.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 2: Calculating the voltage of cells in series</strong></p>

  <p>
    Three ideal 1.5 V cells are connected in series in the same direction.
    Calculate the total voltage.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Write the series rule.</p>
  <p>Total voltage = sum of the individual voltages.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Substitute the values.</p>
  <p>V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate.</p>
  <p>V = 4.5 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> 4.5 V.</p>

  <p><strong>Example 3: Choosing a battery arrangement</strong></p>

  <p>
    A circuit requires approximately 3.0 V. You have two compatible ideal
    1.5 V cells. How should you connect them to obtain the required voltage?
  </p>

  <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the voltage required.</p>
  <p>The circuit requires 3.0 V.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Consider a series connection.</p>
  <p>Series voltages add when the cells face the same direction.</p>

  <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Calculate the total.</p>
  <p>V = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V</p>

  <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Connect the cells in series in the same
  direction.</p>
  `,

  [
    {
      q: "Explain the difference between a primary battery and a secondary battery.",
      hint: "Focus on whether the chemical system is designed to be recharged.",
      steps: [
        "Define a primary battery.",
        "Define a secondary battery.",
        "Explain the difference in how each is used after discharge."
      ],
      ans: "A primary battery is generally designed for single use and is not intended to be recharged. A secondary battery is designed to be recharged and used through repeated charge-discharge cycles.",
      why: "The distinction is based on whether the battery chemistry is designed to be restored by charging."
    },
    {
      q: "A device uses three ideal 1.5 V cells connected in series in the same direction. Calculate the total voltage.",
      hint: "Add the voltages of cells connected in series in the same direction.",
      steps: [
        "Write the three cell voltages.",
        "Calculate 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5.",
        "State the answer in volts."
      ],
      ans: "V = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.5 V",
      why: "The potential differences of ideal cells connected in series in the same direction add."
    },
    {
      q: "Two compatible ideal 1.5 V cells are available. Explain how to connect them to provide approximately 3.0 V and calculate the result.",
      hint: "Choose the arrangement in which the cell voltages add.",
      steps: [
        "Recognise that the required voltage is 3.0 V.",
        "Connect the cells in series with matching orientation.",
        "Add their voltages."
      ],
      ans: "Connect them in series in the same direction. Total voltage = 1.5 + 1.5 = 3.0 V.",
      why: "A series arrangement adds the voltage of each cell when their orientations agree."
    },
    {
      q: "Two compatible ideal cells, each rated at 1.5 V, are connected in parallel with matching polarity. What voltage should the arrangement provide, and how can parallel connection be useful?",
      hint: "Compare the voltage of a parallel arrangement with the voltage of one cell.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the voltage of one cell as 1.5 V.",
        "Apply the rule for compatible equal-voltage cells in parallel.",
        "State a potential advantage of the arrangement."
      ],
      ans: "The arrangement provides approximately 1.5 V. Depending on the cells and circuit, parallel connection can increase available capacity or operating time.",
      why: "Parallel connection does not add the cell voltages; it can allow compatible cells to share the load."
    },
    {
      q: "A student claims that a rechargeable battery can be recharged forever without losing performance. Explain why this claim is incorrect.",
      hint: "Consider what repeated chemical and physical changes do to a battery.",
      steps: [
        "Recognise that secondary batteries can be recharged.",
        "Consider repeated charge-discharge cycles.",
        "Explain how age, heat or unsuitable charging affects performance."
      ],
      ans: "Rechargeable batteries have a limited useful life. Repeated cycling, ageing, heat and unsuitable charging can reduce their capacity and performance.",
      why: "Rechargeability means the chemical system can be restored repeatedly, not that all components remain unchanged forever."
    },
    {
      q: "A portable device requires a higher voltage than one cell can provide. Explain why connecting compatible cells in series may be appropriate, and state one condition for doing so safely.",
      hint: "Consider how series voltage changes and why cell compatibility matters.",
      steps: [
        "Identify the need for a higher voltage.",
        "Explain that series cell voltages add when oriented in the same direction.",
        "State a relevant compatibility or safety condition."
      ],
      ans: "Connecting compatible cells in series in the same direction adds their voltages. The cells should have suitable, compatible ratings and condition, and the arrangement must meet the device's requirements.",
      why: "Series connection raises the total voltage, but incorrect cell selection or connection can damage the cells or device."
    }
  ]
);
